/* NUR 504 Exam 3 — Burns, Shock, Sepsis & DIC question bank.
   Built only from the uploaded Exam 3 material: the Week 7 and Week 8 objectives,
   the Burns / Shock Basics / Shock States / Sepsis-MODS / DIC / Pharmacology decks
   and their speaker notes, and the in-class activities and case studies.
   Every item is Application level or higher per the course question prompt. */
window.NUR504E3_DATA = {
  questions: [
    {"id": "n504e3q001", "exam": 1, "topic": "Burn depth classification", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client is admitted after spilling boiling water on the right thigh. The nurse finds that the wound is pinkish-red, shiny and moist, with several small intact blisters, and the client rates the pain 9/10. How should the nurse document the depth of this injury?", "options": ["Partial-thickness burn", "Superficial burn", "Full-thickness burn", "Deep full-thickness burn"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Moist, shiny, pinkish-red skin with blisters and severe pain is the classic partial-thickness picture: the injury involves the epidermis and dermis, and the surviving nerve endings make it extremely painful. The course material states outright that superficial and full-thickness burns never have blisters, so blistering localizes the depth to partial thickness.", "whyNot": ["", "A superficial burn is dry and has no blisters; the presence of blisters rules this depth out.", "A full-thickness wound is dry, waxy white or leathery and insensitive to pain, not moist, blistered and severely painful.", "This depth extends past the dermis and is insensate; severe pain and intact blisters are inconsistent with it."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q002", "exam": 1, "topic": "Burn depth classification", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client has a burn across the upper back after falling asleep in the sun. The area is markedly red and painful, there are no blisters, and the skin blanches when the nurse presses it. Which documentation is most accurate?", "options": ["Superficial partial-thickness burn with blister formation", "Superficial burn involving the epidermis", "Deep partial-thickness burn requiring grafting", "Full-thickness burn with eschar formation"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Redness, pain, dryness, absence of blisters, and blanching on pressure describe a superficial burn that involves only the epidermis, the depth the material illustrates with mild sunburn. Blanching is the key analytic cue: it shows the dermal capillary network is still perfused and intact.", "whyNot": ["No blisters are present, so documenting blister formation would be inaccurate.", "", "Grafting is associated with full-thickness injury, and nothing here indicates destruction through the dermis.", "Full-thickness wounds are dry, waxy or leathery and do not blanch or hurt; blanching proves the capillary bed is intact."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q003", "exam": 1, "topic": "Full-thickness burn assessment", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client is admitted with a burn to the anterior calf that is dry, waxy white and hard to the touch. Based on this depth of injury, which assessment finding should the nurse anticipate when examining the center of the wound?", "options": ["Severe pain on light touch", "Brisk blanching with capillary refill", "Absence of sensation in the burned area", "Fluid-filled blisters across the wound bed"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "A dry, waxy white, hard wound is full thickness: the injury has destroyed the dermis along with the nerve endings it contains, so the burned area is anesthetized to feeling. Recognizing that a painless burn can be the deepest burn is the analytic point, because the absence of pain is easily misread as a reassuring finding.", "whyNot": ["Severe pain indicates surviving nerve endings, which is characteristic of partial-thickness, not full-thickness, injury.", "Blanching requires an intact dermal capillary bed, which has been destroyed at this depth.", "", "The material states that full-thickness burns never produce blisters."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q004", "exam": 1, "topic": "Burn depth reasoning", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A nurse is triaging four clients in a burn clinic. One client's forearm wound has several tense, fluid-filled blisters. Which conclusion about this wound is best supported by this single finding?", "options": ["The wound is superficial and will heal without scarring", "The wound is full thickness and will need grafting", "The depth cannot be estimated until the blisters are unroofed", "The wound is at least partial thickness in depth"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Because the course material is explicit that superficial and full-thickness burns never blister, the presence of blisters narrows the depth to partial thickness. Blistering therefore excludes both the shallowest and the deepest classifications at once, which is exactly why it is such a useful bedside cue.", "whyNot": ["Superficial burns do not blister, so blistering argues against this depth entirely.", "Full-thickness burns do not blister either; blistering actually argues against this depth.", "The material teaches blistering itself as a depth cue, so the nurse can draw a conclusion now rather than deferring assessment.", ""]},
    {"id": "n504e3q005", "exam": 1, "topic": "Rule of nines", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A 25-year-old woman is admitted with burns to the back of the right arm, the posterior trunk, the front of the left leg, the anterior head and neck, and the perineum. Using the rule of nines, what total body surface area (TBSA) should the nurse calculate as burned?", "options": ["37%", "28%", "36%", "46%"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Each surface is credited separately: the back of one arm is 4.5%, the posterior trunk is 18%, the front of one leg is 9%, the anterior head and neck is 4.5%, and the perineum is 1%. Added together, 4.5 + 18 + 9 + 4.5 + 1 equals 37% TBSA. The analytic trap is crediting a whole region when only the anterior or posterior surface is involved.", "whyNot": ["", "This figure omits roughly a full body region; recount the posterior trunk, which alone is 18%.", "This result leaves out the 1% perineum; the rule of nines assigns the perineum its own percentage.", "This total counts whole regions where only half of each is burned, such as crediting the entire arm rather than only its posterior surface."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q006", "exam": 1, "topic": "Rule of nines", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "An adult sustains partial-thickness burns involving the entire anterior trunk, the entire right arm, and the anterior surface of the left leg. Using the rule of nines, which TBSA percentage should the nurse report to the burn team?", "options": ["27%", "36%", "31.5%", "45%"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "The anterior trunk is 18%, an entire arm is 9%, and the anterior surface of one leg is 9%, giving 18 + 9 + 9 = 36% TBSA. Working region by region and deciding whether the whole region or only one surface is burned is what keeps this calculation accurate.", "whyNot": ["This total credits only 9% for the anterior trunk; the anterior trunk is 18% in the rule of nines.", "", "This figure halves the arm to 4.5%, but the scenario states the entire right arm is burned, which is a full 9%.", "This result credits the entire left leg at 18%, but only the anterior surface is burned, which is 9%."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q007", "exam": 1, "topic": "Rule of nines", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A 28-year-old woman escapes a house fire. On initial assessment the nurse documents burns to the right anterior leg, the entire left leg both anteriorly and posteriorly, and the anterior torso. Using the rule of nines, what is this client's TBSA burned?", "options": ["27%", "36%", "45%", "54%"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "The right anterior leg is 9%, the entire left leg (anterior plus posterior) is 18%, and the anterior torso is 18%, totaling 9 + 18 + 18 = 45% TBSA. A burn of this size far exceeds the 20% threshold at which the material says the response becomes systemic rather than local, so the nurse should anticipate massive fluid shifts.", "whyNot": ["This total credits only one leg surface and omits either the anterior torso or the posterior surface of the left leg.", "This figure credits only the anterior surface of the left leg; the scenario states both surfaces of that leg are burned.", "", "This result credits the entire right leg, but only its anterior surface is burned, adding 9% too many."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q008", "exam": 1, "topic": "Parkland formula", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "An adult weighing 75 kg has partial-thickness and full-thickness burns involving 32% TBSA and arrives immediately after injury. Using the Parkland formula of 4 mL x kg x %TBSA, how much lactated Ringer's solution should the nurse plan to infuse during the first 8 hours after the burn?", "options": ["1,200 mL", "2,400 mL", "9,600 mL", "4,800 mL"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "4 mL x 75 kg x 32% TBSA equals 9,600 mL as the total 24-hour requirement. The Parkland formula allocates half of that volume to the first 8 hours and the remaining half to the next 16 hours, so 4,800 mL is given in the first 8 hours. Distinguishing the 24-hour total from the first-8-hour portion is the step most often missed.", "whyNot": ["This volume is far too small; recheck the multiplication of 4 mL by weight by TBSA before halving.", "This result halves the 24-hour total twice, giving a quarter of the requirement rather than half.", "This is the full 24-hour requirement, not the portion allocated to the first 8 hours.", ""]},
    {"id": "n504e3q009", "exam": 1, "topic": "Parkland formula", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "An adult weighing 80 kg sustains burns involving 30% TBSA and arrives immediately after injury. The prescribed Parkland formula is 4 mL x kg x %TBSA. What initial hourly infusion rate should the nurse calculate for the first 8 hours after the burn?", "options": ["600 mL/hr", "150 mL/hr", "300 mL/hr", "1,200 mL/hr"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "4 mL x 80 kg x 30% TBSA equals 9,600 mL over 24 hours. Half of that, 4,800 mL, belongs to the first 8 hours, so 4,800 divided by 8 equals 600 mL/hr. The material notes this is an initial estimate only; the client's response, especially urine output, guides later titration.", "whyNot": ["", "This rate would deliver only 1,200 mL over 8 hours, a small fraction of the calculated requirement.", "This rate spreads the entire 24-hour volume evenly across 24 hours instead of front-loading half into the first 8 hours.", "This rate would deliver the whole 24-hour requirement in 8 hours, double the intended first-8-hour volume."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q010", "exam": 1, "topic": "Parkland formula timing", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A 70-kg adult with burns involving 40% TBSA arrives in the emergency department 2 hours after the injury occurred. The Parkland formula of 4 mL x kg x %TBSA is prescribed. Which hourly rate should the nurse set to deliver the first half of the resuscitation volume on schedule?", "options": ["466 mL/hr", "933 mL/hr", "700 mL/hr", "1,167 mL/hr"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "4 mL x 70 kg x 40% TBSA equals 11,200 mL over 24 hours, so 5,600 mL belongs to the first 8 hours. The course material specifies that this 8-hour period is counted from the time of injury, not from arrival. With 2 hours already gone, 5,600 mL must be delivered over the remaining 6 hours, which is about 933 mL/hr. Missing the start of the clock leads to systematic under-resuscitation.", "whyNot": ["This rate spreads the first half over 12 hours rather than the 8-hour window the formula specifies.", "", "This rate assumes the 8-hour clock starts on arrival; it starts at the time of injury, so 2 hours have already elapsed.", "This rate would finish the first half in about 5 hours, infusing faster than the formula directs."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q011", "exam": 1, "topic": "Mean arterial pressure", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client being monitored for shock after a major burn has a blood pressure of 102/48 mm Hg. Using the estimate taught in the course, what is this client's mean arterial pressure (MAP)?", "options": ["54 mm Hg", "60 mm Hg", "66 mm Hg", "75 mm Hg"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "MAP is estimated as (systolic + 2 x diastolic) divided by 3, so (102 + 96) divided by 3 equals 66 mm Hg. The diastolic pressure is weighted twice because the heart spends roughly two-thirds of the cardiac cycle in diastole. At 66 mm Hg this client sits just above the 60 to 65 mm Hg at-risk threshold and needs close trending.", "whyNot": ["This value averages the systolic and diastolic readings instead of weighting the diastolic pressure twice.", "This figure underestimates the MAP; recheck that the diastolic value is doubled before dividing by 3.", "", "This result comes from averaging the two pressures as equal halves, which overestimates the MAP."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q012", "exam": 1, "topic": "MAP interpretation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A nurse is reviewing blood pressures for four clients recovering from burn injuries. Using the MAP estimate taught in the course, which client should the nurse escalate first?", "options": ["A client with a blood pressure of 104/64 mm Hg", "A client with a blood pressure of 110/68 mm Hg", "A client with a blood pressure of 96/60 mm Hg", "A client with a blood pressure of 88/50 mm Hg"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "A blood pressure of 88/50 mm Hg gives a MAP of (88 + 100) divided by 3, about 63 mm Hg, which falls into the below 60 to 65 mm Hg at-risk range the course identifies. None of these systolic pressures look alarming at a glance, which is precisely the point: calculating the MAP, rather than reading the systolic number, is what identifies the client whose organ perfusion is threatened.", "whyNot": ["This yields a MAP of about 77 mm Hg, within the normal 70 to 100 mm Hg range.", "This yields a MAP of about 82 mm Hg, comfortably within the normal range.", "This yields a MAP of 72 mm Hg, which is within the normal range.", ""]},
    {"id": "n504e3q013", "exam": 1, "topic": "Electrical burn priorities", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client arrives after an electrical injury sustained while repairing wiring, with entrance and exit wounds on both lower extremities. Which assessment data are the priority for the nurse to obtain?", "options": ["Heart rate and rhythm", "Current range of motion in all extremities", "Respiratory rate and pulse oximetry reading", "Orientation to time, place and person"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Electrical current travels through the body and can disrupt cardiac conduction, so the course material pairs electrical burns with dysrhythmias and the need for an ECG. Heart rate and rhythm are therefore the priority data, because a dysrhythmia can kill this client long before wound depth or mobility becomes relevant.", "whyNot": ["", "Mobility matters for later rehabilitation planning but does not address the immediate life threat from electrical current.", "Oxygenation is always assessed, but electrical current specifically threatens cardiac conduction rather than the airway in this scenario.", "Neurologic assessment is important but secondary to detecting a lethal dysrhythmia."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q014", "exam": 1, "topic": "Electrical burn and acute kidney injury", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A nurse is caring for a client who sustained full-thickness electrical burns to the arms and legs. To detect the kidney complication associated with this mechanism of injury, the nurse should monitor the urine specifically for which substances?", "options": ["Protein and red blood cells", "Hemoglobin and myoglobin", "Potassium and urea", "Free iron and white blood cells"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Electrical injury destroys muscle and deep tissue, releasing myoglobin and hemoglobin into the circulation. The course material explains that myoglobin particles are too large to pass through the nephrons, so they lodge there and damage the kidney, producing acute kidney injury. Detecting these pigments in the urine is an early warning that aggressive fluid resuscitation is needed to protect the kidneys.", "whyNot": ["These suggest glomerular injury or bleeding, not the pigment load released by extensive muscle and tissue destruction.", "", "Potassium and urea are monitored in serum chemistry rather than tracked in urine to detect pigment-induced kidney injury.", "White cells in urine point toward infection, which is not the mechanism of kidney injury after an electrical burn."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q015", "exam": 1, "topic": "Electrical burn complications", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A 65-year-old man has sustained full-thickness electrical burns to both legs and both arms. Which complications should the nurse monitor this client for? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Acute kidney injury", "Cardiac dysrhythmias", "Tissue damage far more extensive than the visible surface wounds", "Bone fractures", "Hyperkalemia", "Hypernatremia", "Fluid volume overload"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "Electrical injury carries a distinct cluster of risks in the course material: current disrupts cardiac conduction and causes dysrhythmias; muscle and tissue destruction releases myoglobin that obstructs the nephrons and causes acute kidney injury; the same cell lysis releases potassium, and the material notes hyperkalemia is especially common with electrical burns; the iceberg effect means the deep injury is far larger than the small surface wounds suggest; and violent muscle contraction during the shock can fracture bone. Early sodium and volume both move in the opposite direction from the two incorrect options.", "whyNot": ["", "", "", "", "", "Sodium is initially low after a burn because it moves out of the extracellular space through leaking vessels, not high.", "The burn client's early problem is hypovolemia from plasma leaking into the interstitium, not circulatory overload."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q016", "exam": 1, "topic": "Inhalation injury recognition", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client is brought to the emergency department after being trapped in a burning building. Which assessment findings should alert the nurse to a possible inhalation injury? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Singed nasal hair, eyebrows and eyelashes", "Black flecks of soot in the sputum", "A hoarse voice with audible stridor", "A new non-productive cough", "Increasing anxiety and a change in mental status", "Blisters on the anterior surface of both legs", "Blanching of the skin on the forearm"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "The material lists singed facial hair and nares, a sooty face or sputum with black flecks, hoarseness or stridor, a non-productive cough, and rising anxiety or altered mental status as the cues that the airway has been injured. These findings matter because the client is at high risk for airway closure and may need rapid intubation or even an emergent tracheotomy. Anxiety and confusion are easily attributed to fear or pain, but in this setting they may signal hypoxia.", "whyNot": ["", "", "", "", "", "Leg blisters describe the depth of a cutaneous burn but say nothing about whether the airway was exposed to heat or smoke.", "Blanching indicates an intact dermal capillary bed in a superficial burn and is unrelated to inhalation injury."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q017", "exam": 1, "topic": "Burn airway priority", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client has sustained full-thickness burns to the face and neck in a kitchen fire. Which nursing action takes priority?", "options": ["Prevent hypothermia", "Assess the blood pressure", "Assess the airway", "Prevent infection"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "The course lists the emergent-phase priorities in order, beginning with securing the airway. Burns to the face and neck place this client at high risk for swelling that can close the airway quickly, and the material stresses the need to intubate early rather than wait. Hypothermia, perfusion and asepsis all matter, but none of them will matter if the airway is lost.", "whyNot": ["Temperature maintenance is a genuine emergent-phase priority, but it follows airway in the course's stated order of care.", "Supporting circulation is the second priority in the emergent phase; the airway is secured first.", "", "Asepsis is an emergent-phase priority but ranks below airway, circulation and temperature."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q018", "exam": 1, "topic": "Airway compromise after burn", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client rescued from a garage fire develops hoarseness, difficulty swallowing, and inspiratory stridor 30 minutes after arrival. Which action should the nurse take first?", "options": ["Encourage coughing and reassess after a sputum specimen is collected", "Offer ice chips to relieve the throat irritation", "Obtain a portable chest radiograph before escalating care", "Activate emergency assistance and prepare for early intubation"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Hoarseness, difficulty managing secretions and inspiratory stridor indicate progressive upper airway edema moving toward obstruction. The material is explicit that these clients are at high risk for airway closure and need to be intubated fast, with an emergent tracheotomy as a possibility. Calling for help and preparing for intubation while the airway is still patent is the only action that gets ahead of the swelling.", "whyNot": ["Collecting a specimen delays definitive airway management while the swelling continues to progress.", "Oral intake is unsafe when the client has difficulty swallowing and risks aspiration.", "Imaging adds information but costs time; the clinical findings already establish an evolving airway emergency.", ""]},
    {"id": "n504e3q019", "exam": 1, "topic": "Burn triage", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Four clients arrive simultaneously after a residential fire. Which client should the nurse assess first?", "options": ["A client with facial burns who has developed a raspy voice and is having difficulty managing secretions", "A client with blistering forearm burns who rates the pain 9/10", "A client with a dry, leathery calf burn who reports little pain at the center of the wound", "A client with superficial shoulder burns who is shivering in wet clothing"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "A raspy voice and trouble managing secretions in a client with facial burns point to evolving airway compromise, which outranks every other finding here. Pain, wound depth and hypothermia all require nursing care, but airway swelling can progress to complete obstruction within minutes, so this client is assessed and escalated first.", "whyNot": ["", "Severe pain from a partial-thickness burn needs prompt analgesia but is not an immediate threat to life.", "This describes a full-thickness burn; the painlessness is expected at that depth and does not indicate instability.", "Heat loss is a real concern and must be addressed, but it does not outrank an evolving airway obstruction."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q020", "exam": 1, "topic": "Emergent phase circulation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client admitted after a large thermal burn injury has a blood pressure of 70/40 mm Hg, a heart rate of 140 beats/min and a respiratory rate of 25 breaths/min. The client is pale and pedal pulses are difficult to palpate. The airway is patent and the client is speaking clearly. Which action will the nurse take first?", "options": ["Check the pulses using a Doppler device", "Start intravenous fluids", "Obtain a complete blood count", "Obtain an electrocardiogram"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "With the airway confirmed patent, the next emergent-phase priority is supporting circulation and perfusion. Hypotension, marked tachycardia, pallor and weak pulses in a large thermal burn reflect the massive plasma loss the material describes, in which increased capillary permeability shifts fluid and protein out of the vascular space. Fluid resuscitation is the only intervention listed that treats that hypovolemia.", "whyNot": ["Confirming a pulse the nurse already knows is weak adds information but does nothing to restore circulating volume.", "", "Laboratory data will be collected, but results take time and will not change the immediate need for volume.", "An ECG is indicated for electrical injury; this is a thermal burn, and the hypotension is driven by fluid loss rather than a rhythm problem."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q021", "exam": 1, "topic": "Emergent phase priorities", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A nurse is planning care for a client in the emergent (resuscitation) phase of a major burn injury. Which actions reflect the priorities of care for this phase? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Securing and protecting the airway", "Supporting circulation and rehydration while working to prevent acute kidney injury", "Maintaining the client's body temperature", "Maintaining asepsis", "Keeping the client comfortable with analgesics", "Providing emotional support to the client and family", "Fitting a compression garment to prevent hypertrophic scarring", "Arranging vocational retraining so the client can resume work"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5], "rationale": "The emergent phase begins at the moment of injury and continues for roughly 24 to 48 hours, and the course lists its priorities as securing the airway, supporting circulation and rehydration while preventing acute kidney injury, maintaining body temperature, maintaining asepsis, providing analgesia, and giving emotional support. Compression garments and vocational retraining are rehabilitative-phase interventions that begin after wound closure, which is what makes them attractive but incorrect here.", "whyNot": ["", "", "", "", "", "", "Compression garments belong to the later rehabilitative work of preventing scars and contractures, not to the first 24 to 48 hours.", "Resuming work and social roles is a goal of the rehabilitative phase, which begins only after wound closure."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q022", "exam": 1, "topic": "Systemic response to burns", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Two clients are admitted from the same fire. One has burns to 15% TBSA and the other to 45% TBSA. The nurse anticipates that the client with 45% TBSA will require far more aggressive management for which reason?", "options": ["A larger burn is more painful, and uncontrolled pain is what drives the hemodynamic instability", "A larger burn is more likely to be full thickness, and only full-thickness burns cause fluid shifts", "Burns exceeding 20% TBSA trigger a systemic response in which mediators released into the circulation affect organs well beyond the wound", "A larger burn requires more dressing changes, which is the main source of fluid and protein loss"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "The course draws a sharp line at 20% TBSA: below it the response stays local, and above it cytokines and other mediators are released into the systemic circulation and the response becomes systemic. That is why a 45% burn is managed as a whole-body illness with massive fluid shifts, while a 15% burn is managed largely as a wound. The material also identifies burns of 20% or more as life threatening.", "whyNot": ["Pain does increase oxygen demand, but the material attributes the systemic deterioration to circulating mediators and capillary leak, not to pain itself.", "Fluid loss begins immediately with any significant burn because heat increases capillary permeability; it is not limited to full-thickness injury.", "", "The plasma loss comes from increased capillary permeability within the vasculature, not from the dressing changes themselves."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q023", "exam": 1, "topic": "Hypothermia in burn injury", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with burns to 38% TBSA has a temperature of 35.6 C (96.1 F) on admission. Which interpretation and action by the nurse is most appropriate?", "options": ["The low temperature is expected and requires no action, because burned tissue cannot generate heat", "The low temperature rules out infection, so antibiotics can safely be withheld", "The client is developing a fever response, so the nurse should prepare to administer an antipyretic", "The loss of skin has impaired thermoregulation; the nurse should warm the room and the intravenous fluids while continuing to monitor closely"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Skin maintains body temperature, so extensive burns leave the client unable to retain heat; maintaining normal body temperature is one of the named emergent-phase priorities, achieved by warming the room and the intravenous fluids. The material adds two cautions that make this analytic rather than routine: loss of skin also blunts the fever response, and hypothermia can itself signal impending shock from sepsis, so a low temperature warrants closer monitoring rather than reassurance.", "whyNot": ["Although damaged skin does impair thermoregulation, a low temperature in a burn client is never dismissed; it requires active warming and ongoing evaluation.", "The material warns that hypothermia can itself be a sign of impending sepsis, so a low temperature does not rule infection out.", "The temperature is below normal, not elevated, so an antipyretic would worsen the heat loss.", ""]},
    {"id": "n504e3q024", "exam": 1, "topic": "Evaluating fluid resuscitation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "An 80-kg adult is receiving lactated Ringer's solution after a major thermal burn. Which finding most directly indicates that resuscitation is meeting the usual minimum adult urine output goal?", "options": ["Urine output of 45 mL/hr", "Urine output of 20 mL/hr", "Heart rate of 118 beats/min", "Increasing edema around the burn wounds"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "The usual minimum adult urine output goal is at least 0.5 mL/kg/hr, which for an 80-kg client is 40 mL/hr; an output of 45 mL/hr meets that target and indicates the kidneys are being perfused. Urine output is the most direct bedside measure of resuscitation adequacy, whereas heart rate and edema can both remain abnormal for other reasons. The nurse still evaluates output alongside the full perfusion picture rather than in isolation.", "whyNot": ["", "For an 80-kg adult the minimum goal is 40 mL/hr, so this output falls well short and suggests continued hypoperfusion.", "Persistent tachycardia suggests the client is still compensating for low volume rather than demonstrating adequate resuscitation.", "Edema reflects ongoing capillary leak and does not demonstrate that the vascular space is adequately filled."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q025", "exam": 1, "topic": "Emergent phase assessment findings", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A nurse is assessing a client who arrived 40 minutes after sustaining burns to 40% TBSA in a house fire. Which findings should the nurse anticipate during this emergent-phase assessment? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Tachycardia with hypotension and decreased cardiac output", "Tachypnea with an increased oxygen demand", "A body temperature below normal", "Decreased sensation over the deepest areas of the burn", "Hoarseness of the voice", "Bradycardia with warm, dry, flushed skin", "Hypertension with bounding peripheral pulses"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "The anticipated emergent-phase findings in the course material include tachycardia, hypotension and decreased cardiac output from plasma loss; tachypnea with increased oxygen demand; hypothermia from the loss of skin; decreased or absent sensation where the burn is deepest; and hoarseness suggesting airway involvement. Bradycardia with warm dry skin and hypertension with bounding pulses both describe the opposite hemodynamic picture from the hypovolemia that dominates the first hours after a major burn.", "whyNot": ["", "", "", "", "", "This pattern is described for neurogenic shock after spinal cord injury, not for the hypovolemia that follows a major burn.", "Plasma loss into the interstitium lowers the blood pressure and weakens pulses; hypertension is not an expected early burn finding."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q026", "exam": 2, "topic": "Capillary leak after burn", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Six hours after sustaining a major burn, a client develops generalized edema, tachycardia and decreased urine output. Which explanation best accounts for this combination of findings?", "options": ["Increased plasma protein concentration is drawing fluid into the circulation", "Increased capillary permeability is shifting fluid and protein out of the vascular space", "The peripheral edema confirms that circulating blood volume is excessive", "Reduced capillary permeability is preventing fluid from reaching the injured tissue"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Heat damage increases capillary permeability, so plasma and protein leak from the vascular space into the interstitium. That produces edema at the same time as it depletes circulating volume, which is why the client is tachycardic and oliguric despite looking swollen. The material identifies hypovolemia as the most profound and direct effect of a burn and attributes it specifically to this protein and fluid shift.", "whyNot": ["Protein is leaving the circulation, not concentrating within it; serum albumin actually falls after a burn.", "", "Edema here reflects fluid trapped in the interstitium while the vascular space is depleted, so it indicates the opposite of overload.", "Heat damage increases rather than reduces capillary permeability, which is why plasma escapes the circulation."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q027", "exam": 2, "topic": "Hyperkalemia after burn", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client admitted 3 hours ago with extensive full-thickness burns has a serum potassium of 6.2 mEq/L. Which interpretation by the nurse is most accurate?", "options": ["The result is unexpected and most likely reflects a hemolyzed specimen", "The elevation reflects potassium moving into cells as the fluid shift resolves", "The potassium released by massive cell destruction and tissue necrosis explains this elevation", "The elevation is caused by the lactated Ringer's solution used for resuscitation"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Immediately after a burn, potassium leaks from lysed cells and necrotic tissue, so hyperkalemia is the expected early picture; the material notes it is especially common with electrical burns and that bicarbonate, glucose and insulin may be needed to correct it. Recognizing this as expected rather than spurious matters, because an untreated potassium of 6.2 mEq/L threatens cardiac conduction in a client who may already be at risk for dysrhythmias.", "whyNot": ["Early hyperkalemia is an expected consequence of burn injury, so the nurse should act on it rather than dismiss it as a laboratory artifact.", "Potassium moving into cells produces the hypokalemia that appears later, usually around days 3 to 10, not early elevation.", "", "The material attributes early hyperkalemia to cell lysis and tissue necrosis, not to the resuscitation fluid."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q028", "exam": 2, "topic": "Hypokalemia in the acute phase", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client is now on day 6 after a 35% TBSA burn. The admission potassium was 6.0 mEq/L; today it is 3.1 mEq/L. Which conclusion should the nurse draw?", "options": ["The falling potassium indicates the burn wounds have become infected", "The original elevated value must have been a laboratory error", "The client is receiving too much potassium-free intravenous fluid and resuscitation should stop", "This downward shift is the expected pattern as fluid shifts occur in the days after the burn"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "The course describes a predictable two-stage potassium course: hyperkalemia immediately after injury from cell destruction, followed by hypokalemia as fluid shifts occur, usually around days 3 to 10. A value of 3.1 mEq/L on day 6 fits that pattern exactly. Knowing the expected trajectory lets the nurse anticipate replacement rather than be surprised by a swing in the opposite direction.", "whyNot": ["Infection is monitored through temperature and white cell trends, not through this expected potassium pattern.", "Both values fit the documented course; the early elevation and the later fall are each expected at their own point in time.", "Resuscitation is guided by perfusion and urine output; the material explains this potassium pattern as a consequence of fluid shifts rather than a reason to stop fluids.", ""]},
    {"id": "n504e3q029", "exam": 2, "topic": "Expected laboratory changes after burn", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client is admitted with burns to 40% TBSA sustained 5 hours ago. Which laboratory findings should the nurse anticipate during this early period? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A low serum sodium level", "An elevated serum potassium level", "An elevated blood glucose level", "An elevated hematocrit", "A low serum albumin level", "A low hematocrit from dilution of the blood", "A low serum potassium level"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "Each early abnormality traces back to the same capillary leak and tissue destruction. Sodium is initially low because it exits the extracellular space through the permeable vessels, and hyponatremia is common during the first week. Potassium is high because it leaks from lysed cells and necrotic tissue. Glucose rises because the hypermetabolic response exhausts glucose stores and the liver increases gluconeogenesis. The hematocrit rises as plasma leaves the red cells behind, and albumin falls as protein escapes with the plasma. Both incorrect options describe the later or opposite direction of change, which is why the timing of the sample matters as much as the value.", "whyNot": ["", "", "", "", "", "Plasma is leaving the vascular space, so the red cells left behind are concentrated and the hematocrit rises rather than falls.", "Hypokalemia develops later, usually around days 3 to 10 as fluid shifts occur; the immediate change is hyperkalemia from cell destruction."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q031", "exam": 2, "topic": "Hemoconcentration after burn", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client admitted with a major burn has a hematocrit of 58%. Beyond indicating plasma loss, why is this value itself a concern for the nurse?", "options": ["An elevated hematocrit impairs microcirculation and produces capillary stasis that can lead to ischemia and necrosis", "An elevated hematocrit shows the client has been over-resuscitated with crystalloid", "An elevated hematocrit confirms active bleeding into the burn wound", "An elevated hematocrit means the client's oxygen-carrying capacity is more than adequate"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "As plasma leaks into the interstitium the red cells left behind are concentrated, so the hematocrit rises. The material identifies the consequence explicitly: thickened blood impairs microcirculation and causes capillary stasis, which leads to ischemia and necrosis. The elevated value is therefore not a reassuring sign of good oxygen-carrying capacity but a marker of sluggish flow through the smallest vessels.", "whyNot": ["", "Over-resuscitation would dilute the blood and lower the hematocrit, not raise it.", "Acute blood loss lowers the hematocrit; this elevation reflects plasma leaving the circulation while red cells remain.", "The red cell mass has not increased; the plasma around it has shrunk, and the thickened blood actually worsens tissue oxygen delivery."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q032", "exam": 2, "topic": "Glucose management in children with burns", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A nurse is caring for a 4-year-old child admitted with a 25% TBSA scald burn. Compared with an adult, which additional metabolic risk should the nurse specifically monitor for in this child?", "options": ["Hypernatremia related to immature kidney function", "Hypoglycemia related to limited glycogen stores", "Hypocalcemia related to rapid bone growth", "Polycythemia related to a higher baseline red cell mass"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "The material states that children are prone to hypoglycemia after a burn because their glycogen stores are limited, in contrast to the hyperglycemia typical of adults whose livers increase gluconeogenesis. Management follows directly from that risk: blood glucose is measured regularly, and early enteral feeding or the addition of dextrose to the electrolyte solution helps prevent it.", "whyNot": ["Sodium falls early after a burn as it shifts out of the extracellular space; the material does not identify hypernatremia as a pediatric burn risk.", "", "Calcium can be affected after a burn, but the material ties the distinct pediatric risk to glucose, not to bone growth.", "The rising hematocrit after a burn reflects plasma loss in clients of any age and is not a pediatric-specific concern."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q033", "exam": 2, "topic": "Case: burn resuscitation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Because she escaped through thick smoke, the nurse is concerned about inhalation injury. Which assessment finding would most strongly support that concern?", "options": ["A respiratory rate of 34 breaths/min", "Anxiety and a report of severe pain", "Black flecks of soot in the sputum with a hoarse voice", "An SpO2 of 93% on a non-rebreather mask"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "The material lists sooty sputum with black flecks and hoarseness among the specific findings that signal the airway has been injured by heat and smoke, along with singed facial hair and nares, stridor, a non-productive cough and rising anxiety or mental status change. These findings matter because they predict airway closure, which is why early intubation is considered before swelling progresses.", "whyNot": ["Tachypnea is expected with pain, anxiety and the increased oxygen demand of a major burn, so it does not point specifically to the airway.", "Anxiety and pain are expected after a house fire and do not by themselves localize the problem to the airway.", "", "This saturation warrants monitoring, but soot and voice change are the specific cues the material lists for airway injury."], "caseId": "an-fire", "caseTitle": "Case 1 · A.N., house fire", "scenario": "A.N. is a 28-year-old woman brought to the emergency department after a house fire. She was asleep when the fire started and escaped through thick smoke. EMS started 100% humidified oxygen at 15 L/min by non-rebreather mask and a 16-gauge IV with lactated Ringer's solution. On arrival her vital signs are BP 100/66, HR 125, RR 34, SpO2 93%. She is alert and oriented x 4 and appears anxious and in pain. Assessment shows burns to the right anterior leg, the entire left leg, and the anterior torso. She weighs 154 lb (70 kg)."},
    {"id": "n504e3q034", "exam": 2, "topic": "Case: burn extent", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "Using the rule of nines, what TBSA should the nurse calculate for A.N.'s burns?", "options": ["27%", "36%", "54%", "45%"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "The right anterior leg is 9%, the entire left leg is 18%, and the anterior torso is 18%, totaling 45% TBSA. This exceeds the 20% threshold at which the material says the burn response becomes systemic, and also the 35% level at which shock is anticipated, so A.N. should be managed as a critically injured client from the outset.", "whyNot": ["This total omits a full 18% region; recount the anterior torso and both surfaces of the left leg.", "This figure credits only the anterior surface of the left leg, but the entire left leg is burned.", "This result credits the entire right leg rather than only its anterior surface, adding 9% too many.", ""], "caseId": "an-fire", "caseTitle": "Case 1 · A.N., house fire", "scenario": "A.N. is a 28-year-old woman brought to the emergency department after a house fire. She was asleep when the fire started and escaped through thick smoke. EMS started 100% humidified oxygen at 15 L/min by non-rebreather mask and a 16-gauge IV with lactated Ringer's solution. On arrival her vital signs are BP 100/66, HR 125, RR 34, SpO2 93%. She is alert and oriented x 4 and appears anxious and in pain. Assessment shows burns to the right anterior leg, the entire left leg, and the anterior torso. She weighs 154 lb (70 kg)."},
    {"id": "n504e3q035", "exam": 2, "topic": "Case: Parkland formula", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A.N. was burned at 0400 and the standard Parkland formula of 4 mL x kg x %TBSA is prescribed for her 45% TBSA burn. How much lactated Ringer's solution should she receive between 0400 and 1200?", "options": ["6,300 mL", "3,150 mL", "12,600 mL", "1,575 mL"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "4 mL x 70 kg x 45% TBSA equals 12,600 mL for the first 24 hours. The Parkland formula gives half in the first 8 hours and half over the next 16 hours, so 6,300 mL is infused between 0400 and 1200, with the remaining 6,300 mL from 1200 to 2000. The 8-hour clock runs from the time of injury, which is why the window closes at 1200 rather than 8 hours after arrival.", "whyNot": ["", "This halves the first-8-hour portion again, delivering only a quarter of the 24-hour requirement.", "This is the full 24-hour requirement; only half of it belongs to the first 8 hours.", "This volume is a small fraction of the requirement; recheck the calculation of 4 mL by 70 kg by 45%."], "caseId": "an-fire", "caseTitle": "Case 1 · A.N., house fire", "scenario": "A.N. is a 28-year-old woman brought to the emergency department after a house fire. She was asleep when the fire started and escaped through thick smoke. EMS started 100% humidified oxygen at 15 L/min by non-rebreather mask and a 16-gauge IV with lactated Ringer's solution. On arrival her vital signs are BP 100/66, HR 125, RR 34, SpO2 93%. She is alert and oriented x 4 and appears anxious and in pain. Assessment shows burns to the right anterior leg, the entire left leg, and the anterior torso. She weighs 154 lb (70 kg)."},
    {"id": "n504e3q036", "exam": 2, "topic": "Case: interpreting burn labs", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A.N.'s admission laboratory results include hemoglobin 20 g/dL, hematocrit 51%, sodium 133 mEq/L, potassium 4.9 mEq/L, glucose 159 mg/dL, BUN 28 mg/dL and creatinine 1.0 mg/dL. Which interpretation is most accurate?", "options": ["The elevated hemoglobin and hematocrit indicate the client has been over-transfused", "The elevated hemoglobin, hematocrit and BUN reflect hemoconcentration from plasma loss, and the glucose is elevated from the hypermetabolic stress response", "The low sodium indicates excessive free water intake and fluids should be restricted", "The normal creatinine rules out any risk of acute kidney injury"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Plasma has leaked out of the vascular space, concentrating what remains, so the hemoglobin, hematocrit and BUN are all elevated together. The glucose rise reflects the hypermetabolic response and increased hepatic gluconeogenesis the material describes, and the mildly low sodium fits the early shift out of the extracellular space. Reading these values as a single pattern of hemoconcentration, rather than as separate abnormalities, is what guides continued aggressive resuscitation.", "whyNot": ["A.N. has received only lactated Ringer's; these values reflect plasma loss concentrating the red cells, not added blood.", "", "Restricting fluid in a 45% TBSA burn would be dangerous; the low sodium reflects the shift out of the extracellular space, and resuscitation must continue.", "A single normal creatinine at admission does not exclude evolving kidney injury, which is why urine output and the trend are monitored closely."], "caseId": "an-fire", "caseTitle": "Case 1 · A.N., house fire", "scenario": "A.N. is a 28-year-old woman brought to the emergency department after a house fire. She was asleep when the fire started and escaped through thick smoke. EMS started 100% humidified oxygen at 15 L/min by non-rebreather mask and a 16-gauge IV with lactated Ringer's solution. On arrival her vital signs are BP 100/66, HR 125, RR 34, SpO2 93%. She is alert and oriented x 4 and appears anxious and in pain. Assessment shows burns to the right anterior leg, the entire left leg, and the anterior torso. She weighs 154 lb (70 kg)."},
    {"id": "n504e3q037", "exam": 2, "topic": "Case: inadequate resuscitation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Eighteen hours after the injury, assistive personnel report that A.N.'s BP is 90/50 mm Hg, HR 130 beats/min, RR 24 breaths/min and temperature 37.2 C, and that her urine output for the past 2 hours totaled 40 mL. Repeat labs show hemoglobin 24 g/dL, hematocrit 59%, sodium 128 mEq/L, BUN 38 mg/dL and creatinine 1.9 mg/dL. What should the nurse conclude?", "options": ["A.N. is being over-resuscitated and the intravenous rate should be reduced", "These findings are expected 18 hours after a burn and require only continued monitoring", "Resuscitation is inadequate; the worsening hemoconcentration, rising creatinine and falling urine output indicate continued hypovolemia and threatened kidney perfusion", "The findings indicate that burn wound sepsis has developed"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "A.N. is putting out 20 mL/hr, well under the 0.5 mL/kg/hr minimum of 35 mL/hr for her 70 kg, while her hematocrit has risen from 51% to 59% and her creatinine from 1.0 to 1.9 mg/dL. Taken together these show the vascular space is still depleted and the kidneys are being hypoperfused, exactly the acute kidney injury the emergent-phase priorities aim to prevent. The expected response is to increase the intravenous rate and recheck, which is what the provider ordered in the case.", "whyNot": ["Over-resuscitation would dilute the blood and increase urine output; here the hematocrit is climbing and output is falling, indicating the opposite problem.", "A creatinine that has nearly doubled alongside oliguria signals evolving kidney injury and requires intervention, not observation.", "", "Sepsis is a later complication and would not explain a rising hematocrit, which points to ongoing plasma loss rather than infection."], "caseId": "an-fire", "caseTitle": "Case 1 · A.N., house fire", "scenario": "A.N. is a 28-year-old woman brought to the emergency department after a house fire. She was asleep when the fire started and escaped through thick smoke. EMS started 100% humidified oxygen at 15 L/min by non-rebreather mask and a 16-gauge IV with lactated Ringer's solution. On arrival her vital signs are BP 100/66, HR 125, RR 34, SpO2 93%. She is alert and oriented x 4 and appears anxious and in pain. Assessment shows burns to the right anterior leg, the entire left leg, and the anterior torso. She weighs 154 lb (70 kg)."},
    {"id": "n504e3q038", "exam": 2, "topic": "Case: evaluating response to therapy", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "By the end of the shift, which assessment finding would best indicate that A.N. is responding to fluid therapy?", "options": ["Respiratory rate 22 breaths/min and BP 120/74 mm Hg", "BP 120/70 mm Hg with urine output of 25 mL/hr for the past 4 hours", "Heart rate 110 beats/min with urine output of 20 mL/hr for the past 4 hours", "BP 104/64 mm Hg with urine output of 40 mL/hr for the past 4 hours"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Urine output is the most direct indicator that resuscitation is reaching the kidneys, and 40 mL/hr meets the 0.5 mL/kg/hr minimum for a 70-kg client, sustained over 4 hours rather than in a single reading. A blood pressure of 104/64 mm Hg gives a MAP of about 77 mm Hg, within the normal range. The distractors pair a normal-looking blood pressure with an inadequate output, which is the trap: pressure can be maintained by compensation while the kidneys remain underperfused.", "whyNot": ["These numbers look reassuring but say nothing about kidney perfusion, the most direct measure of resuscitation adequacy.", "An output of 25 mL/hr is below A.N.'s 35 mL/hr minimum, so perfusion of the kidneys remains inadequate despite the normal pressure.", "Persistent tachycardia with an output of only 20 mL/hr indicates continued hypovolemia.", ""], "caseId": "an-fire", "caseTitle": "Case 1 · A.N., house fire", "scenario": "A.N. is a 28-year-old woman brought to the emergency department after a house fire. She was asleep when the fire started and escaped through thick smoke. EMS started 100% humidified oxygen at 15 L/min by non-rebreather mask and a 16-gauge IV with lactated Ringer's solution. On arrival her vital signs are BP 100/66, HR 125, RR 34, SpO2 93%. She is alert and oriented x 4 and appears anxious and in pain. Assessment shows burns to the right anterior leg, the entire left leg, and the anterior torso. She weighs 154 lb (70 kg)."},
    {"id": "n504e3q039", "exam": 2, "topic": "Case: circumferential burn", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Which complication is this client at particular risk of developing, and which assessments will detect it?", "options": ["Acute compartment syndrome, detected by monitoring for extremity pain and loss of peripheral pulses and sensation", "Curling's ulcer, detected by testing the stool for occult blood", "Paralytic ileus, detected by auscultating the bowel sounds", "Hypothermia, detected by monitoring the core temperature hourly"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "A burn that encircles a limb creates an unyielding ring of tissue, and as resuscitation-related edema accumulates beneath it, pressure rises until perfusion to the distal limb is cut off. The material directs the nurse to report any extremity pain and loss of peripheral pulses or sensation, which are the assessments that detect this before the tissue is lost.", "whyNot": ["", "Curling's ulcer is a real burn complication, but it arises from gastric mucosal ischemia and is unrelated to the circumferential location of this wound.", "A silent bowel is a recognized burn complication, but the encircling wound threatens the limb rather than the gut.", "Burn clients do lose heat, but temperature monitoring will not detect circulation being strangled in a single limb."], "caseId": "circ-burn", "caseTitle": "Case 2 · Circumferential forearm burn", "scenario": "A client is admitted with a circumferential partial- and full-thickness burn encircling the right forearm, sustained when a sleeve caught fire. Fluid resuscitation is underway and the forearm is visibly swelling."},
    {"id": "n504e3q040", "exam": 2, "topic": "Case: compartment syndrome action", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Four hours later the client's fingers are cool, capillary refill is delayed, and the radial pulse is weaker than it was on admission. What should the nurse do first?", "options": ["Apply a compression wrap to limit further swelling", "Notify the burn team immediately and anticipate an escharotomy", "Administer the prescribed analgesic and reassess in 30 minutes", "Lower the arm below heart level to increase arterial inflow"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Cool fingers, delayed capillary refill and a weakening pulse indicate that constricting eschar and progressive edema are compromising distal circulation. This is a time-critical threat to the limb, so the nurse escalates to the burn team at once and anticipates an escharotomy to release the pressure. Each incorrect action either adds pressure, worsens congestion, or spends time the limb does not have.", "whyNot": ["Compression adds external pressure to a limb whose perfusion is already compromised and would worsen the ischemia.", "", "Treating the pain masks the warning sign while the tissue continues to lose its blood supply during the delay.", "Dependent positioning increases venous congestion and worsens the edema driving the problem."], "caseId": "circ-burn", "caseTitle": "Case 2 · Circumferential forearm burn", "scenario": "A client is admitted with a circumferential partial- and full-thickness burn encircling the right forearm, sustained when a sleeve caught fire. Fluid resuscitation is underway and the forearm is visibly swelling."},
    {"id": "n504e3q041", "exam": 2, "topic": "Burn wound sepsis", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with open burn wounds begins to have diarrhea. The nurse notes a normal-to-low temperature, tachycardia, and a white blood cell count of 4,000/mm3. Which action by the nurse is best?", "options": ["Continue to monitor the client", "Increase the temperature in the room", "Prepare to do a workup for sepsis", "Increase the rate of intravenous fluids"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "A low or normal temperature, tachycardia and a white count of 4,000/mm3 together meet the systemic inflammatory response criteria the course teaches, and an open burn wound is a ready source of infection. A low white count is as significant as a high one, and the material warns that hypothermia in a burn client can itself be a sign of impending septic shock. The new diarrhea adds another systemic clue, so the nurse initiates a sepsis workup.", "whyNot": ["This cluster of findings signals a possible systemic infection, which deteriorates quickly; watchful waiting loses critical time.", "Warming the room is appropriate burn care, but it treats heat loss rather than investigating the suspected infection.", "", "Fluids may well be needed, but they would be given as part of sepsis management after the workup is initiated, not instead of it."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q042", "exam": 2, "topic": "Paralytic ileus after burn", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "On the second day after a major burn, the nurse auscultates the abdomen and finds no bowel sounds in any quadrant. The client reports nausea and the abdomen appears distended. Which intervention should the nurse anticipate?", "options": ["Beginning a high-protein oral diet to meet metabolic needs", "Administration of an antidiarrheal medication", "Ambulating the client in the hallway to stimulate peristalsis", "Insertion of a nasogastric tube"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Paralytic ileus is listed among the complications of burns: motor activity of the bowel is impaired without any physical obstruction, so gastric contents accumulate and the abdomen distends. The material pairs the silent bowel directly with nasogastric tube placement, which decompresses the stomach and prevents vomiting and aspiration while motility is absent.", "whyNot": ["Protein needs are genuinely increased after a burn, but oral intake is unsafe when the bowel is not moving and the client is nauseated and distended.", "The problem is absent motility with distension, not diarrhea.", "Early mobility is encouraged in burn care generally, but it does not address gastric contents accumulating above a non-functioning bowel.", ""]},
    {"id": "n504e3q043", "exam": 2, "topic": "Curling's ulcer", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client who sustained burns to 45% TBSA four days ago now has coffee-ground emesis. Which explanation for this development is most consistent with the course material?", "options": ["Reduced plasma volume led to gastric ischemia and necrosis of the gastric mucosa", "The hypermetabolic response has increased gastric acid production as a direct effect of catecholamines", "Bacteria from the burn wound have seeded the stomach and caused an infectious gastritis", "Swallowed soot from the fire has eroded the gastric lining"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Curling's ulcer is described as an acute gastric erosion that complicates severe burns: reduced plasma volume lowers perfusion to the stomach, producing ischemia and necrosis with sloughing of the gastric mucosa. Coffee-ground emesis signals bleeding from that eroded mucosa. Recognizing the mechanism as ischemic explains why adequate fluid resuscitation is itself part of preventing this complication.", "whyNot": ["", "The material explains this complication through ischemic injury from reduced plasma volume rather than through acid production.", "Infection is a major burn complication, but the material attributes this particular lesion to ischemia of the gastric mucosa.", "Soot is a cue for inhalation injury of the airway; the material does not describe it as a cause of gastric erosion."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q044", "exam": 2, "topic": "Burn complications", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A nurse is planning monitoring for a client admitted with burns to 42% TBSA. Which complications should the plan specifically address? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Acute respiratory failure", "Acute kidney injury", "Acute compartment syndrome", "Paralytic ileus", "Infection progressing to sepsis", "Pulmonary edema", "Polycythemia requiring therapeutic phlebotomy", "Hypertensive crisis from catecholamine release"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5], "rationale": "The course lists the complications of burns as acute respiratory failure, distributive shock, acute renal failure, compartment syndrome, paralytic ileus, Curling's ulcer, infection, heart failure, pulmonary edema, dysrhythmias from electrical burns, and contractures. The two incorrect options invert the expected physiology: the raised hematocrit reflects plasma loss rather than true polycythemia, and the blood pressure falls rather than rises as fluid leaves the vascular space.", "whyNot": ["", "", "", "", "", "", "The hematocrit rises from plasma loss, not from red cell overproduction, and it is corrected by fluid resuscitation rather than by removing blood.", "The hemodynamic problem after a major burn is hypotension from plasma loss and decreased cardiac output, not hypertension."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q045", "exam": 2, "topic": "Decreased cardiac output after burn", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client admitted 90 minutes ago with a 38% TBSA burn has a blood pressure that remains near baseline, but the nurse notes a heart rate of 128 beats/min, cool extremities and a urine output of 18 mL in the last hour. Which conclusion is best supported?", "options": ["The near-normal blood pressure indicates adequate cardiac output", "Cardiac output is already falling even though the blood pressure has not yet changed", "The findings are explained by pain and anxiety rather than by the burn injury", "The urine output is adequate for the first hour after a major burn"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "The material states directly that cardiac output decreases before any significant change in blood volume is evident, which is why the blood pressure can look deceptively reassuring early. Tachycardia, cool extremities and oliguria are the compensatory and perfusion cues that reveal the falling output. Acting on this pattern rather than waiting for hypotension is what prevents the progression to shock and acute kidney injury.", "whyNot": ["Cardiac output falls before any significant change in blood volume is evident, so a maintained pressure does not confirm adequate output.", "", "Pain and anxiety can raise the heart rate, but they do not explain cool extremities together with oliguria, which point to poor perfusion.", "An output of 18 mL/hr falls below the 0.5 mL/kg/hr minimum and signals hypoperfusion of the kidneys."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q046", "exam": 2, "topic": "Acute phase of burn care", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client is 48 hours post-burn and the massive fluid shift has resolved. Which nursing focus best reflects the priorities of the phase the client has now entered?", "options": ["Calculating the remaining Parkland volume and completing fluid resuscitation", "Fitting compression garments and planning the return to work and social roles", "Continued cardiovascular and respiratory assessment with attention to nutrition, wound care, pain control, asepsis and psychosocial needs", "Securing the airway and establishing large-bore intravenous access"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "The acute phase begins about 36 to 48 hours after injury, when the fluid shift resolves, and lasts until wound closure is complete. Its nursing considerations are continued assessment and maintenance of the cardiovascular and respiratory systems along with nutrition, wound care, pain control, asepsis and psychosocial intervention. Matching the intervention to the phase prevents both premature rehabilitation planning and repeating emergent-phase work.", "whyNot": ["Formula-guided resuscitation belongs to the emergent phase, which ends at about the time the fluid shift resolves.", "These belong to the rehabilitative phase, which begins only once the wounds are closed.", "", "These are first-priority emergent-phase actions that should already have been accomplished."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q047", "exam": 2, "topic": "Rehabilitative phase", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client whose burn wounds have fully closed is being prepared for discharge. Which statement by the nurse best describes the goals of the phase the client is entering?", "options": ["This phase lasts about 12 to 14 days and ends when the wounds epithelialize", "This phase is complete once the client can perform range of motion without assistance", "This phase concentrates on completing fluid resuscitation and preventing acute kidney injury", "This phase focuses on psychosocial adjustment, preventing scars and contractures, and resuming pre-burn work, family and social roles, and may last months to years"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "The rehabilitative or restorative phase begins with wound closure and ends when the client achieves their highest level of functioning. Its nursing focus is psychosocial adjustment, prevention of scars and contractures, and resumption of pre-burn activity including work, family and social roles. The material stresses that it may take years or even last a lifetime depending on the extent of injury, and that each program is individualized.", "whyNot": ["That description fits the acute phase; the rehabilitative phase begins where wound closure leaves off.", "The phase ends when the client reaches the highest level of functioning, which encompasses far more than range of motion.", "These are emergent-phase priorities from the first 24 to 48 hours after injury.", ""]},
    {"id": "n504e3q048", "exam": 2, "topic": "Compression garments", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client recovering from deep partial-thickness burns to the arm asks why a compression garment has been prescribed. Which response by the nurse is most accurate?", "options": ["It helps prevent contractures and tight hypertrophic scars while inhibiting venous stasis and edema in areas where lymph flow is reduced", "It applies pressure that stops bleeding from the healing wound bed", "It keeps the dressings in place so they do not require changing as often", "It warms the limb to prevent the hypothermia common after a burn"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "The course describes the compression garment as a non-surgical intervention applied to prevent contractures and tight hypertrophic scars, and also to inhibit venous stasis and edema in areas with decreased lymph flow. Explaining both purposes matters for adherence, since these garments must be worn consistently over a long period to work.", "whyNot": ["", "The garment is prescribed for scar and contracture management, not for hemostasis in a closed wound.", "Securing dressings is not the stated purpose; the garment addresses scarring, contracture and edema.", "Hypothermia is managed by warming the room and intravenous fluids during the emergent phase, not by a compression garment later on."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q030", "exam": 2, "topic": "Burn rehabilitation", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client whose burn wounds have closed is beginning the rehabilitative phase of care. Which interventions should the nurse include in the plan? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Promoting physical mobility and range of motion to prevent contractures", "Applying a compression garment to limit hypertrophic scarring", "Providing psychosocial support and addressing changes in body image", "Arranging follow-up care and referrals", "Continuing wound care and client education", "Calculating and infusing the remaining Parkland formula volume", "Inserting a nasogastric tube to decompress the stomach"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "Burn rehabilitation centers on wound care, education, follow-up care, referrals and psychosocial support, and the phase's nursing considerations are psychosocial adjustment, prevention of scars and contractures, and resumption of pre-burn work, family and social roles. Promoting mobility and fitting compression garments both serve the prevention of contractures and tight hypertrophic scars. The two incorrect options are genuine burn interventions taken from the emergent and early acute phases, which is what makes them tempting once the phases blur together.", "whyNot": ["", "", "", "", "", "Formula-guided fluid resuscitation belongs to the emergent phase in the first 24 hours, long before wound closure.", "Decompression is indicated for the paralytic ileus of the early acute period, not for a client whose wounds have healed."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q049", "exam": 2, "topic": "Pain management during wound care", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A nurse is preparing to change the dressings on a client's deep partial-thickness burns. The client was medicated for pain 4 hours ago and now rates pain at rest as 4/10. Which action best reflects the course's approach to pain control for this procedure?", "options": ["Perform the dressing change now and medicate afterward if the client reports pain", "Pre-medicate the client and time the dressing change for when the analgesic has taken effect", "Withhold analgesia so the client can accurately report changes in wound sensation", "Explain that pain is expected and use distraction techniques instead of medication"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "The material lists pain control among the non-surgical nursing interventions for burn care and directs the nurse specifically to pre-medicate before dressing changes. Dressing changes on partial-thickness burns expose intact nerve endings and are predictably severe, so analgesia is timed to peak during the procedure rather than given in response to pain afterward.", "whyNot": ["Treating pain after the fact leaves the client to endure a predictably severe procedure without coverage.", "", "Sensation is assessed as part of determining depth, but withholding analgesia for a painful procedure is not supported.", "Non-pharmacologic support is valuable alongside analgesia, but it does not replace pre-medication for a procedure this painful."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q050", "exam": 2, "topic": "Infection prevention in burn care", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with extensive open burn wounds is in the acute phase of care. Which nursing actions support the goals of infection control and wound healing? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Maintaining asepsis during all wound care", "Monitoring the complete blood count and white cell differential frequently", "Performing hand hygiene before and after every client contact", "Removing invasive lines and catheters as soon as they are no longer needed", "Monitoring for a low or falling white cell count together with a low temperature as possible signs of sepsis", "Restricting dietary protein to reduce the metabolic demand on the healing wound", "Leaving the wounds open to room air and allowing the room to cool so bacteria grow more slowly"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "Infection control and wound healing are named nursing interventions for burn care, supported by maintaining asepsis, frequent monitoring of the complete blood count and differential for signs of infection, hand hygiene, and removing invasive devices once they are no longer needed. The course also warns that a falling white count and a low temperature can signal sepsis rather than reassure. The two incorrect options work against established burn priorities: protein needs are increased, and body temperature must be actively maintained.", "whyNot": ["", "", "", "", "", "Burn clients have markedly increased protein needs for wound healing; restricting protein would impair it.", "Burn clients cannot regulate temperature and require a warm room and warm fluids; deliberately cooling them risks hypothermia."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q051", "exam": 3, "topic": "Early recognition of shock", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client is 8 hours out from abdominal surgery. The blood pressure remains in the client's usual range, but the heart rate is now 122 beats/min, the respiratory rate is 28 breaths/min, and the client has become newly restless. Which action should the nurse take first?", "options": ["Document the findings and recheck the vital signs at the next scheduled time", "Administer the prescribed anxiolytic to settle the restlessness", "Perform a focused assessment of perfusion and escalate the concern", "Wait for the blood pressure to fall before notifying the provider"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Tachycardia, tachypnea and new restlessness are the cues the course identifies as early or compensatory shock, and the material states plainly that early shock hides behind a normal blood pressure. The nurse's move is to assess perfusion directly, support oxygenation and circulation, escalate the concern and reassess. Waiting for hypotension means intervening only once the body's compensation has already been exhausted.", "whyNot": ["These cues may represent early shock, which can progress quickly; delaying reassessment wastes the compensated window.", "Sedating the client treats a symptom that may be an early sign of inadequate cerebral perfusion and masks the deterioration.", "", "Blood pressure is a late sign; waiting for hypotension means acting after compensation has already failed."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q052", "exam": 3, "topic": "Early signs of hypovolemic shock", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is monitoring a postoperative client at risk for hypovolemic shock. Which assessment finding indicates an early sign of shock?", "options": ["Blood pressure 110/58 mm Hg", "Respiratory rate 12 breaths/min", "First-degree heart block", "Heart rate 120 beats/min"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Tachycardia is the earliest compensatory sign of shock, driven by sympathetic stimulation attempting to maintain cardiac output as volume falls. The course material names tachycardia as the first symptom and emphasizes that the blood pressure is still in range at this stage. Recognizing a rising heart rate against a normal pressure is what allows intervention during the compensated phase.", "whyNot": ["This pressure is still within range; hypotension is a late sign and its absence does not rule out early shock.", "This rate is normal; it is tachypnea, not a normal or slow rate, that accompanies early shock.", "A conduction delay is not among the compensatory findings of early shock; the sympathetic response speeds the heart rather than slowing conduction.", ""]},
    {"id": "n504e3q053", "exam": 3, "topic": "Compensation and blood pressure", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A nursing student asks why a client who is clearly deteriorating still has a normal blood pressure. Which explanation by the nurse is most accurate?", "options": ["Sympathetic, renin-angiotensin and antidiuretic hormone responses temporarily maintain the pressure, so hypotension appears only after compensation fails", "Blood pressure stays normal until at least half of the circulating volume has been lost", "A normal blood pressure means the client is perfusing adequately and shock can be excluded", "The blood pressure cuff is unreliable in shock, so the reading should be disregarded"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "When perfusion falls, the sympathetic nervous system raises heart rate and contractility and causes vasoconstriction, the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system retains sodium and water, and antidiuretic hormone retains water. Together these temporarily maintain blood pressure and perfusion, which is why the course states that shock may be present before the blood pressure falls. Hypotension marks the failure of compensation rather than the onset of shock.", "whyNot": ["", "The material explains the maintained pressure through compensatory mechanisms, not through a specific percentage of volume loss.", "Shock is a perfusion problem before it is a blood pressure problem, so a normal reading does not establish adequate perfusion.", "The reading is real; the issue is that compensation is holding it up, not that the measurement is inaccurate."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q054", "exam": 3, "topic": "Oxygen delivery and demand", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with shock has a blood pressure within the normal range but continues to show signs of poor perfusion. Which factors should the nurse consider when evaluating this client's oxygen delivery and demand? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Cardiac output, which is heart rate multiplied by stroke volume", "The hemoglobin level", "The arterial oxygen saturation", "Fever, which increases oxygen demand", "Pain and agitation, which increase oxygen demand", "Shivering and increased work of breathing, which increase oxygen demand", "The serum sodium level, which determines how much oxygen the blood can carry", "The blood pressure, which is the single best measure of oxygen delivery"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5], "rationale": "Oxygen delivery is the product of cardiac output, hemoglobin and arterial oxygen saturation, so delivery falls if any part of that chain fails. Demand rises independently with fever, pain, shivering, increased work of breathing and agitation, which is why a client can decompensate without any change in blood pressure. The two incorrect options name a value with no role in the equation and repeat the misconception the lecture is built to correct.", "whyNot": ["", "", "", "", "", "", "Oxygen-carrying capacity depends on hemoglobin and saturation; sodium plays no part in the delivery equation.", "The course's central point is that oxygen delivery depends on more than blood pressure, and that pressure can be normal while delivery is inadequate."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q055", "exam": 3, "topic": "Lactate trend", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client being treated for shock had an initial lactate of 4.6 mmol/L. After treatment, the repeat lactate is 2.4 mmol/L. How should the nurse interpret this change?", "options": ["The lactate is still abnormal, so the treatment has had no effect", "The falling lactate suggests that tissue perfusion is improving", "The falling lactate confirms the client no longer has shock", "The change indicates the client has developed a respiratory rather than a metabolic problem"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Lactate rises when tissues are hypoperfused and cells shift to anaerobic metabolism, and the course stresses that the trend is more useful than a single value. A falling lactate over time signals improving perfusion and is described as a good prognostic sign. The nurse still reads it alongside the full clinical picture, because an elevated lactate supports the story of poor perfusion without diagnosing it on its own.", "whyNot": ["The material teaches that the trend carries more information than any single value, and this trend is moving in the right direction.", "", "Lactate supports the perfusion picture but does not diagnose or exclude shock by itself.", "Lactate elevation contributes to a high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis; a falling value does not signal a respiratory disorder."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q056", "exam": 3, "topic": "Severe tissue hypoxia", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "The nurse is reviewing laboratory results for a client with septic shock. Which value alerts the nurse to severe tissue hypoxia?", "options": ["PaCO2 58 mm Hg", "Partial thromboplastin time 64 seconds", "Lactate 9.0 mmol/L", "Potassium 2.8 mEq/L"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "A lactate of 9.0 mmol/L means cells have shifted to anaerobic metabolism on a large scale, which is the definition of severe tissue hypoxia; the course notes that severe lactic acidosis is a level above 5 mmol/L against a normal range of roughly 0.6 to 1.8 mmol/L. The other abnormal values each point to a different organ problem but none of them measures whether the tissues are receiving oxygen.", "whyNot": ["A raised PaCO2 reflects a ventilation problem and respiratory acidosis rather than the anaerobic metabolism of hypoperfused tissue.", "A prolonged PTT suggests a coagulation problem such as DIC, which is a complication of sepsis rather than a direct measure of tissue oxygenation.", "", "Hypokalemia requires attention but does not indicate how well the tissues are being oxygenated."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q057", "exam": 3, "topic": "Compensatory mechanisms", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client's tissue perfusion has begun to decrease after a significant blood loss. Which physiologic responses should the nurse expect as the body attempts to compensate? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["An increase in heart rate", "An increase in myocardial contractility", "Peripheral vasoconstriction", "Renin release with sodium and water retention", "Antidiuretic hormone release with water retention", "Bradycardia from increased vagal tone", "Peripheral vasodilation to improve tissue blood flow"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "The course maps compensation along three pathways: the sympathetic nervous system raises heart rate and contractility and constricts vessels, the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system retains sodium and water to expand volume, and antidiuretic hormone retains water. Together they temporarily maintain blood pressure and perfusion. The two incorrect options describe the physiology of neurogenic and distributive shock, where these protective responses are lost rather than mounted.", "whyNot": ["", "", "", "", "", "Bradycardia occurs in neurogenic shock, where sympathetic tone is lost; it is not a compensatory response to blood loss.", "Compensation constricts peripheral vessels to shunt blood centrally; vasodilation is the problem in distributive shock, not a compensatory response."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q058", "exam": 3, "topic": "Refractory stage of shock", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is caring for a client in the refractory stage of shock. Which intervention is most likely for this client?", "options": ["Admission to a rehabilitation hospital for ambulatory retraining", "Collaboration with a home care agency for return to home", "Enrollment in a cardiac transplantation program", "Discussion with the family and provider regarding palliative care"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "In the refractory stage, severe tissue hypoxia has produced ischemia and necrosis, toxic metabolites have accumulated, and multiple organ dysfunction is present with a poor response to treatment. Because the goal of reversing shock is no longer attainable, care shifts toward goals of care and comfort, which makes a palliative care discussion the appropriate intervention.", "whyNot": ["Rehabilitation presumes recovery; in the refractory stage organ failure is established and treatment is no longer working.", "Discharge planning of this kind does not fit a client whose organs are failing and who is not responding to treatment.", "Transplantation is not the trajectory described for refractory shock, where multiple organ systems are failing.", ""]},
    {"id": "n504e3q059", "exam": 3, "topic": "Interpreting a single set of vital signs", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Assistive personnel express concern about a postoperative client whose blood pressure is 90/60 mm Hg, heart rate 80 beats/min and respirations 22 breaths/min. What is the appropriate nursing action?", "options": ["Compare these vital signs with the last several readings", "Request that the surgeon come to see the client", "Increase the rate of the intravenous fluids", "Reassess the vital signs using different equipment"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "One set of numbers means little in isolation, and this particular set is not internally alarming: the heart rate is normal, which argues against the compensatory tachycardia of early shock. The course directs the nurse to compare against the trend before acting, because this may simply be the client's baseline pressure. Trending, then escalating with specifics, is the pattern the material teaches throughout the shock lectures.", "whyNot": ["", "Escalation may well be needed, but calling before knowing whether these numbers represent a change leaves the provider without the information that matters.", "Giving fluid is a treatment decision that should follow assessment; this pressure may be the client's baseline.", "Nothing suggests an equipment problem, and repeating the measurement does not answer the real question of whether these values represent a change."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q060", "exam": 3, "topic": "Delegation in shock", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A postoperative client is admitted to the intensive care unit with hypovolemic shock. Which nursing action is appropriate for the nurse to delegate to experienced assistive personnel?", "options": ["Obtain vital signs every 15 minutes", "Measure hourly urine output", "Check the oxygen saturation", "Assess the level of alertness"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Measuring and recording hourly urine output is a task within the scope of assistive personnel, and the nurse retains responsibility for interpreting what the number means for perfusion. Vital signs, oxygen saturation and level of alertness in an unstable client all require the nurse's assessment, because each reading must be weighed against the trend and acted on immediately if it changes.", "whyNot": ["Monitoring an unstable client's vital signs requires the nurse's interpretation of each set against the trend.", "", "Assessing oxygenation in an unstable client and acting on the value requires nursing judgment.", "Mental status is a sensitive indicator of cerebral perfusion in shock and its assessment cannot be delegated."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q061", "exam": 3, "topic": "Risk for hypovolemic shock", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "The nurse is reviewing the assignment for the shift. Which client is at greatest risk for hypovolemic shock?", "options": ["A client with kidney failure", "A client with arthritis taking daily acetaminophen", "A client with esophageal varices", "A client with pain from a kidney stone"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Esophageal varices are dilated, fragile vessels that can rupture and bleed massively, and gastrointestinal hemorrhage is listed among the direct causes of hypovolemic shock. The analytic step is distinguishing a condition that can cause sudden major volume loss from conditions that are serious for other reasons but do not empty the vascular space.", "whyNot": ["Kidney failure is associated with fluid retention rather than with the acute volume loss that causes hypovolemic shock.", "Acetaminophen does not carry a bleeding risk of the kind that would produce hypovolemic shock.", "", "A kidney stone causes severe pain but is not a source of the major volume loss required for hypovolemic shock."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q062", "exam": 3, "topic": "Hypovolemic shock assessment", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client is admitted with hypovolemic shock following a gastrointestinal bleed. Which assessment findings should the nurse expect? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Tachycardia with a weak, thready pulse", "Pale, cool, clammy skin", "Flat neck veins", "Skin tenting", "Falling urine output", "Distended jugular veins", "Warm, flushed skin with bounding pulses"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "Hypovolemic shock is a volume problem, so the body constricts peripherally and the findings follow: tachycardia with a weak pulse, pale cool clammy skin, flat neck veins, skin tenting and falling urine output. The course highlights neck veins as the discriminating sign, flat in hypovolemic shock and distended in cardiogenic and obstructive shock, and warm flushed skin as the early exception that points instead toward a distributive cause.", "whyNot": ["", "", "", "", "", "Distended neck veins occur in cardiogenic and obstructive shock, where blood backs up; in hypovolemic shock the veins are flat because the tank is empty.", "This pattern appears early in septic and anaphylactic shock from vasodilation, the opposite of the vasoconstriction seen in hypovolemia."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q063", "exam": 3, "topic": "Cardiogenic shock mechanism", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client develops hypotension, cool skin and crackles throughout both lung fields following a myocardial infarction. Which mechanism explains these findings?", "options": ["Loss of circulating blood volume", "Widespread peripheral vasodilation", "Obstruction of pulmonary blood flow", "Reduced ventricular pumping ability"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Myocardial damage reduces ventricular contractility, so stroke volume and cardiac output fall and the client becomes hypotensive with cool, clammy skin. Pressure behind the failing left ventricle backs up into the lungs, producing the crackles. The course frames this as the pump problem, in contrast to obstructive shock where the muscle itself is healthy but flow is blocked.", "whyNot": ["Volume loss would produce flat neck veins and clear lungs, not the pulmonary congestion heard here.", "Vasodilation produces warm flushed skin; this client's skin is cool, indicating vasoconstriction in response to a failing pump.", "An obstruction such as a pulmonary embolism causes sudden dyspnea and hypoxemia but would not produce crackles from backed-up left-sided pressures.", ""]},
    {"id": "n504e3q064", "exam": 3, "topic": "Fluids in cardiogenic shock", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client in cardiogenic shock after a large myocardial infarction has a MAP of 58 mm Hg, crackles in both bases and jugular venous distention. A new graduate nurse suggests a rapid 1,000 mL normal saline bolus. Which response by the experienced nurse is most appropriate?", "options": ["Fluids are given with caution in cardiogenic shock, because the problem is a failing pump rather than an empty tank, and a large bolus can worsen the pulmonary congestion", "A rapid bolus is appropriate, because every form of shock is treated first with aggressive fluid resuscitation", "Fluids should be withheld entirely, because clients in cardiogenic shock never require intravenous fluid", "The bolus is appropriate once the crackles have resolved"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "In cardiogenic shock the ventricle cannot move the volume it already has, and the crackles and jugular venous distention show that blood is backing up behind it. The course directs inotropes and restoration of coronary flow for this state, with fluids given cautiously rather than as a rapid bolus. This is the clearest illustration of the lecture's central rule that stabilizing measures must be matched to the cause.", "whyNot": ["", "The course is explicit that the cause decides the treatment; a volume strategy that helps hypovolemic shock can harm a failing pump.", "The material says fluids are given with caution, not that they are contraindicated.", "Waiting for crackles to clear misses the point: the congestion reflects the failing ventricle, and the plan should address contractility and coronary flow."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q065", "exam": 3, "topic": "Cardiac tamponade", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client who sustained blunt chest trauma develops jugular venous distention, muffled heart sounds and hypotension. Which intervention should the nurse anticipate?", "options": ["Rapid infusion of two units of packed red blood cells", "Pericardiocentesis", "Needle decompression of the second intercostal space", "Initiation of an antiarrhythmic infusion"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Jugular venous distention, muffled heart sounds and hypotension make up Beck's triad, which points to cardiac tamponade, a form of obstructive shock in which fluid in the pericardial sac prevents the ventricles from filling. The fix is to remove the obstruction by pericardiocentesis or a pericardial window. The heart muscle itself is healthy, which is what separates obstructive from cardiogenic shock.", "whyNot": ["Transfusion addresses hemorrhage; this triad indicates that the heart is being compressed from outside and cannot fill.", "", "Needle decompression treats a tension pneumothorax, which presents with absent unilateral breath sounds and tracheal deviation rather than muffled heart sounds.", "The problem is mechanical compression preventing ventricular filling, not a rhythm disturbance."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q066", "exam": 3, "topic": "Tension pneumothorax", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client admitted after a motor vehicle crash becomes acutely dyspneic. The nurse finds absent breath sounds on the right, tracheal deviation toward the left, and a blood pressure of 78/44 mm Hg. Which action should the nurse anticipate?", "options": ["Pericardiocentesis to relieve pressure around the heart", "Anticoagulation for a suspected pulmonary embolism", "Needle decompression followed by chest tube insertion", "Immediate intubation and a rapid fluid bolus"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Absent unilateral breath sounds with tracheal deviation and hypotension identify a tension pneumothorax, an obstructive shock state in which accumulating air compresses the heart and great vessels so the ventricles cannot fill. The course pairs this presentation directly with needle decompression and chest tube placement. Removing the blockage is what reverses the shock; supportive measures alone will not.", "whyNot": ["Pericardiocentesis treats tamponade, which presents with muffled heart sounds and distended neck veins rather than unilateral absent breath sounds with tracheal deviation.", "An embolism causes sudden dyspnea and hypoxemia but does not push the trachea to the opposite side or abolish breath sounds on one side.", "", "Neither intervention relieves the trapped air that is compressing the heart and great vessels; the pressure must be released."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q067", "exam": 3, "topic": "Distributive shock mechanism", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with a severe infection has hypotension, warm skin and bounding pulses. Which change explains these findings?", "options": ["Increased systemic vascular resistance", "Reduced circulating volume from hemorrhage", "Impaired ventricular filling from external compression", "Widespread vasodilation with reduced vascular resistance"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Septic shock is a form of distributive shock in which vessels dilate throughout the body, dropping systemic vascular resistance and allowing blood to pool in the veins while capillaries also leak. Warm skin and bounding pulses can occur early for exactly that reason, which makes this the exception the course warns about: these clients can look deceptively well-perfused while their effective circulating volume is falling.", "whyNot": ["Increased resistance would produce cool, constricted extremities, the opposite of the warm skin and bounding pulses found here.", "Hemorrhage causes hypovolemic shock with pale, cool, clammy skin and a weak pulse.", "External compression describes obstructive shock and would be accompanied by distended neck veins and muffled heart sounds.", ""]},
    {"id": "n504e3q068", "exam": 3, "topic": "Neurogenic shock", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A 24-year-old arrives after a motorcycle crash unable to move or feel his legs. His BP is 84/48 mm Hg with a MAP of 60 mm Hg, heart rate 52 beats/min, and his skin is warm and dry. There is no visible bleeding. Which conclusion and action are most appropriate?", "options": ["The findings fit neurogenic shock, but because this is trauma the nurse must also assess for occult bleeding", "The findings fit hypovolemic shock, so the bradycardia should be treated as an equipment artifact", "The findings fit septic shock, so blood cultures and antibiotics are the priority", "The warm, dry skin indicates the client is perfusing adequately and needs only spinal precautions"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Hypotension with bradycardia and warm, dry skin after a spinal injury points to neurogenic shock, in which sympathetic tone is lost so vessels dilate and the heart slows. The course stresses that this is the exception where the heart rate is slow rather than fast. It equally stresses that in trauma, hemorrhage can coexist, so the nurse maintains spinal precautions, assesses for bleeding, supports the blood pressure, treats the bradycardia as ordered and keeps the client warm.", "whyNot": ["", "The bradycardia is a genuine finding and a key discriminator; hypovolemic shock produces tachycardia, not a slow rate.", "Nothing here suggests infection, and the mechanism of injury points to loss of sympathetic tone after spinal cord injury.", "Warm dry skin here reflects loss of vasomotor tone, not good perfusion; the MAP of 60 mm Hg is at the at-risk threshold."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q069", "exam": 3, "topic": "Exceptions across shock states", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A nurse is teaching new graduates how to use assessment findings to identify the cause of a client's shock. Which statements correctly describe the exceptions that point toward a specific cause? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Warm, flushed skin early in the course suggests septic or anaphylactic shock", "Bradycardia with hypotension suggests neurogenic shock", "Flat neck veins suggest hypovolemic shock", "Distended neck veins suggest cardiogenic or obstructive shock", "Cold, clammy, mottled skin identifies the cause as obstructive shock", "Oliguria identifies the cause as cardiogenic shock", "Altered mental status identifies the cause as septic shock"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3], "rationale": "The course teaches that hypotension, tachycardia, tachypnea, altered mental status, cold clammy mottled skin and oliguria are common to most shock states, so they establish that the client is in shock without naming the cause. The discriminating exceptions are warm flushed skin early in septic and anaphylactic shock, bradycardia in neurogenic shock, and the state of the neck veins, flat when the tank is empty and distended when blood is backing up. The exceptions are what point to the cause.", "whyNot": ["", "", "", "", "Cold clammy skin is common to most shock states and therefore does not point to any one cause.", "Falling urine output occurs across shock states as perfusion drops and does not discriminate between causes.", "Altered mental status accompanies progressive shock of any cause and is not specific to sepsis."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q070", "exam": 3, "topic": "Nursing priorities in shock", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A nurse is caring for a 70-kg client being treated for shock. Which finding should prompt the nurse to notify the provider based on the monitoring parameters taught in the course?", "options": ["Hourly urine output of 50 mL", "Hourly urine output of 25 mL", "A MAP of 72 mm Hg", "A capillary refill time of 2 seconds"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "The course sets the hourly urine output goal at a minimum of 0.5 mL/kg/hr, which is 35 mL/hr for a 70-kg client, and directs the nurse to notify the provider when output falls below the expected threshold. An output of 25 mL/hr is below that minimum and indicates the kidneys are not being adequately perfused, making it the finding that requires escalation while the MAP and capillary refill remain reassuring.", "whyNot": ["This exceeds the 35 mL/hr minimum for a 70-kg client and suggests the kidneys are being perfused.", "", "This falls within the normal 70 to 100 mm Hg range and above the at-risk threshold.", "A brisk refill time suggests adequate peripheral perfusion rather than deterioration."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q071", "exam": 3, "topic": "Case: recognizing anaphylaxis", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Which conclusion about M.M.'s condition is best supported by the assessment findings?", "options": ["He is having a localized allergic reaction that will resolve without treatment", "He is in hypovolemic shock from insensible losses during yard work", "He is having an anaphylactic reaction, a form of distributive shock", "He is having a panic attack related to the stings"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Generalized urticaria with flushing and itching, edema of the lip and tongue, wheezing in all fields, hypotension, tachycardia, dizziness and drowsiness together describe anaphylaxis. The course classifies anaphylactic shock as a type of distributive shock: the IgE-mediated reaction causes vasodilation, capillary leak and bronchospasm, so blood pools, effective circulating volume falls and the airway narrows at the same time.", "whyNot": ["Hives are generalized, the airway is swelling and he is hypotensive and drowsy; the reaction is systemic, not localized.", "Dehydration would not produce urticaria, lip and tongue edema or wheezing.", "", "Anxiety does not cause urticaria, airway edema or measurable hypotension, and treating this as panic would delay life-saving epinephrine."], "caseId": "mm-ana", "caseTitle": "Case 3 · M.M., bee sting reaction", "scenario": "M.M., a 46-year-old man, presents to the emergency department after being stung three times by bees while doing yard work. He had localized pain and swelling at first and was not concerned because he has never reacted to a sting before. About 15 minutes later he became dizzy and short of breath, and his girlfriend drove him in after seeing hives develop. Vital signs: BP 100/60, HR 112, RR 32, SpO2 93% on room air, temperature 37.1 C. He has mild edema of the lip and tongue and reports a metallic taste. Moderate wheezing is heard in all fields with minimal retractions. He is drowsy and reports dizziness. His skin shows a generalized urticarial rash with flushing and he reports itching. He denies any history of allergies and takes furosemide for mild hypertension."},
    {"id": "n504e3q072", "exam": 3, "topic": "Case: anaphylaxis priority", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "The provider orders oxygen, epinephrine 0.5 mg IM now, diphenhydramine 50 mg IV, hydrocortisone 200 mg IV and a 1,000 mL normal saline bolus. Which order should the nurse carry out first?", "options": ["Diphenhydramine 50 mg IV", "Hydrocortisone 200 mg IV", "The 1,000 mL normal saline bolus", "Epinephrine 0.5 mg IM"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "The course states the rule plainly: epinephrine first, and antihistamines do not reverse shock. Epinephrine acts on all three problems at once, constricting dilated vessels, strengthening cardiac contraction and opening the airways. Antihistamines, bronchodilators and corticosteroids follow as adjuncts, and the material cautions that they must never delay epinephrine.", "whyNot": ["Antihistamines are adjuncts that do not reverse shock, and giving one first delays the only drug that does.", "Corticosteroids are adjuncts given after epinephrine and have no immediate effect on the vasodilation and airway swelling.", "Fluids support the blood pressure and are part of care, but they do not reverse the bronchospasm or airway edema the way epinephrine does.", ""], "caseId": "mm-ana", "caseTitle": "Case 3 · M.M., bee sting reaction", "scenario": "M.M., a 46-year-old man, presents to the emergency department after being stung three times by bees while doing yard work. He had localized pain and swelling at first and was not concerned because he has never reacted to a sting before. About 15 minutes later he became dizzy and short of breath, and his girlfriend drove him in after seeing hives develop. Vital signs: BP 100/60, HR 112, RR 32, SpO2 93% on room air, temperature 37.1 C. He has mild edema of the lip and tongue and reports a metallic taste. Moderate wheezing is heard in all fields with minimal retractions. He is drowsy and reports dizziness. His skin shows a generalized urticarial rash with flushing and he reports itching. He denies any history of allergies and takes furosemide for mild hypertension."},
    {"id": "n504e3q073", "exam": 3, "topic": "Case: epinephrine dose", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse prepares the ordered epinephrine 0.5 mg IM from a multiple-dose vial labeled epinephrine 1 mg/mL, 30 mL. How many milliliters should the nurse administer per dose?", "options": ["0.5 mL", "0.05 mL", "1 mL", "5 mL"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "With a concentration of 1 mg/mL, a 0.5 mg dose is contained in 0.5 mL. The course emphasizes checking the concentration, route and dose of epinephrine every single time, because the drug comes in different concentrations for different routes and mixing them up is a recognized source of serious medication error. For anaphylaxis the intramuscular route into the anterolateral thigh is used, and the dose may be repeated as needed.", "whyNot": ["", "This is one-tenth of the required volume and would deliver only 0.05 mg.", "This volume would deliver 1 mg, twice the ordered dose.", "This volume would deliver 5 mg, ten times the ordered dose."], "caseId": "mm-ana", "caseTitle": "Case 3 · M.M., bee sting reaction", "scenario": "M.M., a 46-year-old man, presents to the emergency department after being stung three times by bees while doing yard work. He had localized pain and swelling at first and was not concerned because he has never reacted to a sting before. About 15 minutes later he became dizzy and short of breath, and his girlfriend drove him in after seeing hives develop. Vital signs: BP 100/60, HR 112, RR 32, SpO2 93% on room air, temperature 37.1 C. He has mild edema of the lip and tongue and reports a metallic taste. Moderate wheezing is heard in all fields with minimal retractions. He is drowsy and reports dizziness. His skin shows a generalized urticarial rash with flushing and he reports itching. He denies any history of allergies and takes furosemide for mild hypertension."},
    {"id": "n504e3q074", "exam": 3, "topic": "Case: ongoing anaphylaxis assessment", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "As the nurse continues to assess M.M., which findings would be of greatest concern? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Hoarseness", "A high-pitched cough", "Wheezing", "Abdominal cramping", "Nausea and vomiting", "Joint swelling and arthralgia"], "correct": [0, 1, 2], "rationale": "The most concerning findings are the ones that signal the airway is narrowing. The course lists hoarse voice, throat tightness and stridor under airway, and wheeze and shortness of breath under breathing, and a high-pitched cough reflects the same narrowing of the upper airway. Gastrointestinal symptoms such as cramping, nausea and vomiting do occur in anaphylaxis, but they do not warn of imminent obstruction, so they do not drive the immediate response.", "whyNot": ["", "", "", "Cramping occurs in anaphylaxis and should be documented, but it does not signal the airway closing.", "These are recognized features of anaphylaxis but are not the findings that predict imminent airway obstruction.", "Joint symptoms are not among the airway, breathing or circulation findings the course identifies in anaphylaxis."], "caseId": "mm-ana", "caseTitle": "Case 3 · M.M., bee sting reaction", "scenario": "M.M., a 46-year-old man, presents to the emergency department after being stung three times by bees while doing yard work. He had localized pain and swelling at first and was not concerned because he has never reacted to a sting before. About 15 minutes later he became dizzy and short of breath, and his girlfriend drove him in after seeing hives develop. Vital signs: BP 100/60, HR 112, RR 32, SpO2 93% on room air, temperature 37.1 C. He has mild edema of the lip and tongue and reports a metallic taste. Moderate wheezing is heard in all fields with minimal retractions. He is drowsy and reports dizziness. His skin shows a generalized urticarial rash with flushing and he reports itching. He denies any history of allergies and takes furosemide for mild hypertension."},
    {"id": "n504e3q075", "exam": 3, "topic": "Case: autoinjector teaching", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "M.M. is discharged with a prescription for an epinephrine autoinjector. Which statements by M.M. indicate that teaching has been effective? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["“I will not put my fingers or hand over the orange tip of the injector.”", "“My girlfriend needs to call 911 after I use the autoinjector.”", "“I should press it firmly into my outer thigh and hold it in place until it clicks and the time is up.”", "“I need to carry two autoinjectors with me.”", "“The best place to administer the injection is in my stomach.”", "“I should only give myself an injection if I have difficulty breathing.”", "“I need to take an antihistamine before using the autoinjector.”"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3], "rationale": "Correct autoinjector technique in the course material is to remove the safety cap with the orange tip pointing toward the thigh, press firmly into the outer thigh until it clicks, hold for about 3 seconds or as the device directs, then call 911 and lie down, recognizing that a second dose may be needed, which is why two devices are carried. Keeping fingers clear of the orange tip prevents an accidental injection into the hand. The incorrect statements name the wrong site, set the threshold for use too high, and reverse the order of epinephrine and antihistamine.", "whyNot": ["", "", "", "", "The autoinjector is given into the outer, anterolateral thigh, not the abdomen.", "Anaphylaxis can present with hypotension, dizziness or collapse before breathing changes; waiting for dyspnea delays treatment.", "Epinephrine comes first; antihistamines are adjuncts and must never delay it."], "caseId": "mm-ana", "caseTitle": "Case 3 · M.M., bee sting reaction", "scenario": "M.M., a 46-year-old man, presents to the emergency department after being stung three times by bees while doing yard work. He had localized pain and swelling at first and was not concerned because he has never reacted to a sting before. About 15 minutes later he became dizzy and short of breath, and his girlfriend drove him in after seeing hives develop. Vital signs: BP 100/60, HR 112, RR 32, SpO2 93% on room air, temperature 37.1 C. He has mild edema of the lip and tongue and reports a metallic taste. Moderate wheezing is heard in all fields with minimal retractions. He is drowsy and reports dizziness. His skin shows a generalized urticarial rash with flushing and he reports itching. He denies any history of allergies and takes furosemide for mild hypertension."},
    {"id": "n504e3q076", "exam": 4, "topic": "SIRS screening", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A nurse is screening four clients at the start of the shift. Which client screens positive and requires the nurse to look for both a source of infection and evidence of organ dysfunction?", "options": ["A client with a temperature of 37.2 C, heart rate 88 beats/min and WBC 9,000/mm3", "A client with a suspected wound infection, a temperature of 38.6 C, heart rate 104 beats/min and WBC 14,200/mm3", "A client with no suspected infection, a temperature of 37.0 C and a heart rate of 94 beats/min", "A client with a healing surgical incision, blood pressure 138/82 mm Hg and WBC 10,400/mm3"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "The screen is positive when two or more systemic inflammatory response criteria are present along with a suspected infection. This client meets three: a temperature above 38 C, a heart rate above 90 beats/min and a white count above 12,000/mm3, with a suspected wound infection as the source. The course is clear that a positive screen flags inflammation rather than diagnosing sepsis, and the nurse's next step is to look for the infection and for organ dysfunction.", "whyNot": ["None of these values meets a systemic inflammatory response criterion, so this client does not screen positive.", "", "A single criterion with no suspected infection does not meet the threshold of two or more criteria plus suspected infection.", "Blood pressure is not a systemic inflammatory response criterion, and this white count is within normal limits."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q077", "exam": 4, "topic": "SIRS without infection", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client admitted with acute pancreatitis and no evidence of infection has a temperature of 38.3 C, heart rate 110 beats/min and respiratory rate 24 breaths/min. Which interpretation by the nurse is most accurate?", "options": ["These findings confirm the client has sepsis and antibiotics should be started immediately", "The findings are unrelated to the pancreatitis and point to an occult infection that must be found", "The client meets systemic inflammatory response criteria, which can occur without infection in conditions such as pancreatitis, trauma and burns", "The findings can be disregarded because the client has no suspected infection"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "The course states that the systemic inflammatory response syndrome can occur with or without infection, naming trauma, burns and pancreatitis as non-infectious triggers. Inflammation that was beneficial while confined to the injury site becomes harmful once it enters the bloodstream and produces widespread tissue and vascular changes. It is when infection is the cause of acute organ dysfunction that the picture becomes sepsis.", "whyNot": ["Sepsis requires organ dysfunction caused by infection; these criteria alone, with no infection, do not establish it.", "Pancreatitis is named in the course as a trigger of this response in its own right, so the findings are explained by the known diagnosis.", "", "The response is described as harmful and potentially deadly whether or not infection is present, and it can progress to multiple organ dysfunction."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q078", "exam": 4, "topic": "Septic shock criteria", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with sepsis has received adequate fluid resuscitation. Which combination of findings indicates that the client has progressed to septic shock?", "options": ["A temperature of 39.1 C with a white count of 19,000/mm3", "A positive blood culture with a rising procalcitonin", "A lactate of 2.4 mmol/L that falls to 1.6 mmol/L after a fluid bolus", "A need for vasopressors to maintain a MAP of at least 65 mm Hg together with a lactate above 2 mmol/L despite adequate fluids"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "The course defines septic shock as sepsis plus the need for vasopressors to keep the MAP at 65 mm Hg or above, plus a lactate above 2 mmol/L, both persisting despite adequate fluid resuscitation. The two qualifiers matter: the client must have already received fluid, and both the vasopressor requirement and the lactate elevation must remain. Fever, leukocytosis and positive cultures describe the infection, not the circulatory failure.", "whyNot": ["These reflect inflammation and infection but say nothing about the circulatory failure that defines septic shock.", "These confirm infection but do not establish the vasopressor requirement and lactate elevation that define shock.", "A lactate that corrects with fluid alone shows the client responded to volume, which is the opposite of the persistent shock state.", ""]},
    {"id": "n504e3q079", "exam": 4, "topic": "Cultures before antibiotics", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse plans to administer an antibiotic to a client newly admitted with septic shock. The client already has a patent intravenous line and a current set of vital signs. What action will the nurse take first?", "options": ["Ensure that blood cultures were drawn", "Administer the antibiotic", "Insert an intravenous line", "Take the client's vital signs"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Blood cultures are obtained before antimicrobials whenever possible so the organism can still be identified, which later allows the antibiotic to be narrowed from broad-spectrum coverage. The course pairs this with an equally firm caution that culture collection must not delay treatment: in septic shock or probable sepsis, antimicrobials are ideally given within 1 hour, because delayed administration is linked to increased mortality.", "whyNot": ["", "Giving the antibiotic before cultures are drawn can prevent the organism from being identified, which the material warns against when cultures can be obtained promptly.", "The scenario states that a patent line is already in place.", "A current set of vital signs has already been obtained."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q080", "exam": 4, "topic": "Escalating a positive sepsis screen", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A postoperative client has a temperature of 38.4 C, heart rate 104 beats/min, respiratory rate 24 breaths/min and new restlessness. The blood pressure is 118/70 mm Hg. What should the nurse do first?", "options": ["Recheck the vital signs in 4 hours", "Notify the provider using SBAR and follow the sepsis protocol", "Give acetaminophen and reassess the temperature", "Encourage incentive spirometry"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "This is a positive screen: the client meets systemic inflammatory response criteria, has a plausible postoperative source of infection, and has a new mental status change that suggests organ dysfunction. The course directs the nurse to escalate now rather than wait for hypotension, communicating with SBAR and saying the word sepsis so the hour-1 steps can begin. The normal blood pressure is reassuring only on the surface, because it is a late sign.", "whyNot": ["Sepsis can progress within hours; a 4-hour delay forfeits the window in which treatment is most effective.", "", "Treating the fever addresses one criterion while leaving the underlying process unaddressed and may mask further deterioration.", "Pulmonary hygiene is good postoperative care but does not respond to a positive sepsis screen with a mental status change."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q081", "exam": 4, "topic": "Sepsis bundle", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A nurse is teaching new staff about the unit's sepsis bundle. Which statements should the nurse include? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A bundle is a group of interventions that work best when delivered together", "Measure the lactate level and remeasure it if elevated", "Obtain blood cultures before antimicrobials when possible, without delaying treatment", "Begin rapid administration of crystalloid for hypotension or hypoperfusion", "Start vasopressors if hypotension persists after fluid resuscitation", "Measure fibrinogen levels as part of the initial bundle", "Delay antimicrobials until culture results identify the organism"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "The course describes the immediate steps grouped into the sepsis bundle as measuring lactate and remeasuring it if elevated, obtaining blood cultures before antimicrobials, giving antimicrobials on time, giving crystalloid for hypotension or hypoperfusion, and starting vasopressors if hypotension persists. The underlying idea is that these interventions work best delivered together as a set. Fibrinogen belongs to the coagulation workup, and waiting for culture results inverts the principle of empiric treatment followed by narrowing.", "whyNot": ["", "", "", "", "", "Fibrinogen is followed when disseminated intravascular coagulation is suspected, not as one of the immediate sepsis bundle steps.", "Antimicrobials are given empirically and on time, then narrowed once results return; waiting for results increases mortality."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q083", "exam": 4, "topic": "Middle-stage sepsis", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client admitted with sepsis 10 hours ago now has a blood pressure and respiratory rate that have improved, pink and warm extremities, and no cyanosis. However, the white count has fallen from 18,000 to 9,000/mm3, urine output has decreased to 10 mL/hr, the oxygen saturation has drifted down 3%, and the client seems slightly less oriented than earlier. How should the nurse interpret these findings?", "options": ["The client is improving and the reduced urine output reflects appropriate fluid conservation", "The normalized vital signs indicate the sepsis has resolved and monitoring can be reduced", "The client appears better superficially, but the falling white count, dropping urine output and subtle mental status change indicate the illness is progressing", "The falling white count shows the infection has been successfully treated by the antibiotics"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "The course describes a deceptive middle stage in which cardiac output rises to compensate for capillary leak, so blood pressure and respiratory rate improve and the client looks good with pink, warm extremities and no cyanosis. Underneath, the white count falls because neutrophils are being burned up faster than bands can replace them, urine output falls toward absent, the oxygen saturation drifts down and mental status changes subtly. Recognizing deterioration behind a reassuring appearance is the entire point of this stage.", "whyNot": ["Falling urine output signals organ hypoperfusion; it is never interpreted as a sign of improvement in sepsis.", "The course specifically warns that the client looks good at this stage while cellular deterioration continues underneath.", "", "The material explains the drop as neutrophils being consumed faster than bands can be produced, which reflects an exhausted defense rather than a cured infection."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q084", "exam": 4, "topic": "White cell findings in sepsis", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with a suspected infection and new confusion has a white blood cell count of 9,800/mm3 with 14% bands. A colleague states that sepsis can be excluded because the white count is normal. Which response by the nurse is most accurate?", "options": ["The colleague is correct; a normal white count reliably excludes sepsis", "The elevated band count indicates a viral rather than a bacterial process", "Sepsis can only be diagnosed once the white count exceeds 12,000/mm3", "A normal white count does not rule out sepsis, and the elevated bands indicate a left shift as mature neutrophils are consumed"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "The course notes that in sepsis the white count may be high, normal or low, and states explicitly that a normal white count does not rule sepsis out. A left shift, in which band forms rise as mature neutrophils are used up, is listed as a separate marker of the infectious process. Combined with new confusion, which may be the first sign of sepsis in an older adult, this client warrants escalation rather than reassurance.", "whyNot": ["The course states directly that a normal white count does not rule out sepsis.", "The material describes rising bands as a left shift reflecting consumption of mature neutrophils in the setting of infection, not as a marker of viral illness.", "That value is one systemic inflammatory response criterion, not a diagnostic requirement; sepsis is organ dysfunction caused by infection.", ""]},
    {"id": "n504e3q085", "exam": 4, "topic": "MODS", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with septic shock now has a creatinine that has risen from 1.0 to 2.8 mg/dL, worsening hypoxemia requiring mechanical ventilation, and rising AST, ALT and bilirubin. Which conclusion should the nurse draw?", "options": ["The client has developed multiple organ dysfunction syndrome, defined as progressive dysfunction of two or more organ systems", "The client has developed disseminated intravascular coagulation", "These changes are expected adverse effects of the vasopressor infusion", "The findings indicate the client is in the compensatory stage of shock"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Multiple organ dysfunction syndrome is defined in the course as progressive dysfunction of two or more organ systems, and the material pairs specific laboratory trends with specific organs: rising creatinine and BUN for the kidneys, rising AST, ALT and bilirubin for the liver, and severe hypoxemia from lung injury. This client shows three systems failing at once, and the course notes that mortality rises with each additional failing organ.", "whyNot": ["", "That diagnosis rests on a coagulation pattern of falling platelets and fibrinogen with rising PT, INR and D-dimer, which is not described here.", "Vasopressors can cause ischemia, but this simultaneous deterioration across the kidneys, lungs and liver reflects progressive organ dysfunction from the shock state.", "Established organ dysfunction places the client well past compensation, where the body is still successfully masking the problem."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q086", "exam": 4, "topic": "Sepsis risk factors", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "The charge nurse is reviewing a unit census. Which clients warrant heightened concern for sepsis? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A 54-year-old woman receiving chemotherapy for breast cancer", "An 86-year-old man with acute onset confusion", "A 44-year-old man with alcohol use disorder and diabetes", "A 72-year-old woman with an indwelling urinary catheter and a central line", "A 68-year-old man with a chronic pressure injury over the sacrum", "A 41-year-old man with a closed femur fracture", "A 44-year-old woman with anxiety and infertility"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "The course lists the risk factors for sepsis and multiple organ dysfunction as age 65 and over or under 1 year, recent trauma or surgery, indwelling devices such as urinary catheters and central lines, immunosuppression from chemotherapy, steroids or transplant, cancer, open wounds including burns and pressure injuries, malnutrition, and chronic illness such as diabetes and alcohol use disorder. Acute confusion in an older adult is singled out as a warning sign that may itself signal sepsis. A closed fracture provides no portal of entry, which is what separates it from an open wound.", "whyNot": ["", "", "", "", "", "A closed fracture does not breach the skin, so it lacks the portal of entry and immune compromise that drive sepsis risk.", "Neither condition suppresses immune defenses or creates a route for infection."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q087", "exam": 4, "topic": "DIC laboratory pattern", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client being treated for a severe infection develops widespread bruising and persistent bleeding after venipuncture. Which laboratory findings would support a diagnosis of disseminated intravascular coagulation? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A decreased platelet count", "A decreased fibrinogen level", "An elevated D-dimer", "A prolonged PT with an increased INR", "A prolonged PTT", "A falling hemoglobin and hematocrit", "An increased fibrinogen level", "An increased platelet count"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5], "rationale": "The laboratory pattern in disseminated intravascular coagulation reflects clotting supplies being consumed: platelets and fibrinogen fall, PT, INR and PTT rise because the factors needed to form a clot have been exhausted, D-dimer rises as the body breaks the clots back down, and hemoglobin and hematocrit fall with ongoing bleeding. The two incorrect options invert the two values that fall most characteristically. No single test makes the diagnosis; the pattern is trended alongside the clinical picture.", "whyNot": ["", "", "", "", "", "", "Fibrinogen is consumed by widespread clotting, so it falls rather than rises.", "Platelets are used up by the widespread clotting, which is precisely why the client then bleeds."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q088", "exam": 4, "topic": "DIC recognition in sepsis", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with sepsis has new oozing from the intravenous site and new petechiae across both arms. The platelet count has fallen from 180,000 to 60,000/mm3. What should the nurse do first?", "options": ["Document the findings and recheck in the morning", "Notify the provider, because these findings may signal disseminated intravascular coagulation", "Remove the intravenous catheter and restart it in the other arm", "Apply a warm compress to the intravenous site"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "New bleeding together with a sharply falling platelet count in a septic client suggests disseminated intravascular coagulation, and the course notes that new petechiae or oozing in a septic patient can be its first sign. Sepsis is the most common cause, because microthrombi are part of the septic cascade. Escalating immediately matters because the clotting and bleeding continue until the trigger is treated, and every additional needle stick creates another site that will not stop bleeding.", "whyNot": ["This combination signals an acute, rapidly evolving complication; waiting overnight allows bleeding and microthrombi to progress unchecked.", "", "Restarting the line creates an additional puncture site that will bleed, adding risk without addressing the cause.", "A compress treats local irritation and does nothing for a systemic coagulation failure."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q089", "exam": 4, "topic": "DIC bleeding precautions", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with disseminated intravascular coagulation following sepsis is admitted to the unit. Which nursing actions should be included in the plan of care? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Provide oral care with a soft toothbrush or sponge and avoid flossing", "Use an electric razor only", "Avoid intramuscular injections and unnecessary needle sticks", "Cluster laboratory draws and hold pressure at puncture sites for at least 5 minutes", "Avoid rectal temperatures and enemas", "Reposition gently, relieve pressure points and pad the side rails", "Administer prescribed pain medication by the intramuscular route", "Encourage daily flossing to prevent gingival infection"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5], "rationale": "Because clotting factors and platelets have been consumed, every needle stick and pressure point becomes a bleeding risk. The course directs gentle oral care with a soft brush or sponge and no flossing, electric razors only, avoidance of intramuscular injections and extra sticks, clustering of laboratory draws with at least 5 minutes of pressure afterward, no rectal temperatures or enemas, and gentle repositioning with padded side rails. The incorrect options each deliberately breach fragile tissue in a client who cannot stop bleeding.", "whyNot": ["", "", "", "", "", "", "An intramuscular injection creates a deep bleeding site in a client who cannot form a stable clot.", "Flossing traumatizes fragile gums and will provoke bleeding that is difficult to stop."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q090", "exam": 4, "topic": "DIC treatment priorities", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client develops disseminated intravascular coagulation after septic shock. Blood products have been ordered. Which principle should guide the nurse's understanding of the treatment plan?", "options": ["Heparin is given to every client with this diagnosis to stop the clotting cascade", "Blood products alone will resolve the condition once the deficits are replaced", "Treating the underlying sepsis is essential, because the clotting and bleeding continue until the trigger is addressed", "The condition is a primary bleeding disorder that should be managed independently of the sepsis"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "The course frames this condition as an acquired syndrome that is never the first problem: widespread clotting is triggered by another serious illness, and sepsis is its most common cause. Blood products such as platelets, plasma and cryoprecipitate replace what has been used up and buy time, and heparin is reserved for when thrombosis predominates, but only treating the trigger stops the cycle. That is why source control and antimicrobials for the sepsis remain central to the plan.", "whyNot": ["Heparin is used only when thrombosis is the predominant problem, and it requires close monitoring for bleeding.", "Products buy time by replacing what has been consumed, but the cycle continues until the trigger is treated.", "", "It is an acquired syndrome that is never the first problem; the course directs the nurse always to look for the trigger."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q091", "exam": 4, "topic": "First-line vasopressor", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A 54-year-old client with septic shock remains hypotensive after initial fluid resuscitation, with a MAP of 54 mm Hg. Which prescribed medication should the nurse anticipate initiating as the first-line vasopressor?", "options": ["Milrinone", "Furosemide", "Nitroglycerin", "Norepinephrine"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Norepinephrine is the first-line vasopressor in septic shock. It acts predominantly on alpha-1 receptors to constrict arteries and veins, raising systemic vascular resistance and blood pressure, with a mild beta-1 effect that slightly increases contractility. The course sequences therapy as fluids first, then norepinephrine, then vasopressin added rather than escalating norepinephrine, then epinephrine if the MAP remains inadequate.", "whyNot": ["This agent is not the first-line vasopressor for septic shock and would not be expected to raise the MAP in this situation.", "A diuretic removes volume, which would worsen hypotension in a client who has just been fluid resuscitated for shock.", "This drug dilates vessels and would lower the blood pressure further in a client who is already hypotensive.", ""]},
    {"id": "n504e3q092", "exam": 4, "topic": "Vasopressor extravasation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with septic shock is receiving norepinephrine through a forearm intravenous line. The MAP is 62 mm Hg and the skin around the intravenous site is pale, cool and swollen. Which action should the nurse take first?", "options": ["Stop the infusion at that site and notify the provider", "Increase the norepinephrine rate to bring the MAP to goal", "Apply a warm compress and continue the infusion while monitoring", "Flush the line with normal saline to confirm patency"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Blanching, coolness, swelling or pain at the site indicate extravasation, and a vasopressor that leaks into tissue constricts the vessels there so the skin can die. The course directs the nurse to stop the infusion at that site, notify the provider, follow protocol for the antidote phentolamine, and elevate, mark and monitor the area. The second priority follows immediately: establish new access quickly and restart the drug, because the client is also not receiving the full dose and the MAP is below goal.", "whyNot": ["", "Increasing the rate drives more vasoconstrictor into the tissue and accelerates the damage while still not reaching the circulation reliably.", "Continuing the infusion at a site that is clearly extravasating allows the tissue injury to progress.", "Flushing pushes more of the drug into the tissue, and the course directs that these lines are never flushed or bolused."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q093", "exam": 4, "topic": "Vasopressin", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client in septic shock has vasopressin added to an existing norepinephrine infusion. Two hours later the nurse notes that the urine output has decreased. How should the nurse interpret this finding?", "options": ["The decrease indicates the client has developed acute kidney injury and the drug should be held", "The decrease is an expected effect, because vasopressin acts on kidney receptors to reabsorb water", "The decrease indicates the vasopressin dose is too low and should be titrated upward", "The decrease is unrelated to the vasopressin and reflects inadequate fluid resuscitation"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Vasopressin works through two non-adrenergic receptors: V1 constricts vessels to raise blood pressure, and V2 causes the kidneys to reabsorb water. A decrease in urine volume is therefore an expected and even therapeutic effect, accompanied by increased urine osmolality. The nurse still monitors sodium and intake and output, since water retention and low sodium are listed among the effects to watch for, and evaluates perfusion using the fuller picture rather than urine output alone.", "whyNot": ["Reduced output is an anticipated effect of this drug, so holding it without evaluating the whole perfusion picture would remove needed blood pressure support.", "", "Vasopressin is usually given at a fixed low dose rather than titrated like norepinephrine.", "Fluid status is always assessed, but this drug has a direct and expected antidiuretic effect through its action on the kidneys."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q094", "exam": 4, "topic": "Evaluating dobutamine", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client in cardiogenic shock is started on dobutamine. Which finding indicates that the drug is producing the desired effect?", "options": ["The heart rate rises to 130 beats/min", "New premature ventricular contractions appear on the monitor", "Urine output increases and the extremities become warmer", "The blood pressure falls from 88/50 to 78/44 mm Hg"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Dobutamine stimulates beta-1 receptors so the heart contracts harder, raising cardiac output. Better cardiac output shows up at the bedside as improved perfusion: more urine, warmer extremities and clearer mentation. The course separates these therapeutic endpoints from the drug's adverse effects of tachycardia, premature ventricular contractions, dysrhythmias and hypotension, which is what makes this question an evaluation of the response rather than recall of the mechanism.", "whyNot": ["Tachycardia is a listed adverse effect of this drug, not evidence that it is working.", "Premature ventricular contractions and dysrhythmias are adverse effects to report, not therapeutic endpoints.", "", "Hypotension from the drug's mild vasodilating effect is an adverse effect requiring reassessment."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q095", "exam": 4, "topic": "Dobutamine safety", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A provider prescribes dobutamine for a client with low-output heart failure. Before starting the infusion, which action is most important for the nurse to take?", "options": ["Obtain a baseline serum potassium level", "Arrange for the client to be weighed on a bed scale", "Ensure the client has a urinary catheter with a urometer in place", "Confirm that hypovolemia has been corrected"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "The course directs the nurse to correct hypovolemia before starting dobutamine. The drug increases contractility but also produces mild beta-2 vasodilation, so giving it to a client whose tank is empty can drop the blood pressure further. Once started, care includes continuous ECG and blood pressure monitoring, hourly urine output, short-term use, and reporting chest pain.", "whyNot": ["Electrolytes are monitored in critical illness, but the course names correcting volume status as the specific prerequisite for this drug.", "A weight is useful for ongoing fluid assessment but is not the safety step that must precede this infusion.", "Hourly urine output is monitored during the infusion, but establishing adequate volume comes first.", ""]},
    {"id": "n504e3q096", "exam": 4, "topic": "Dopamine infusion rate", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client weighing 176 lb has a prescription for dopamine at 8 mcg/kg/min. The available infusion contains 400 mg in 250 mL. At what rate should the nurse program the pump? Round to the nearest whole number.", "options": ["24 mL/hr", "15 mL/hr", "30 mL/hr", "38 mL/hr"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "The client weighs 176 divided by 2.2, which is 80 kg, so the dose is 8 x 80, or 640 mcg/min. The concentration is 400,000 mcg in 250 mL, which is 1,600 mcg/mL. The rate is therefore (640 x 60) divided by 1,600, which equals 24 mL/hr. The course also notes that dopamine is not the first-line agent for septic shock because it carries a higher risk of tachyarrhythmias and potentially increased mortality.", "whyNot": ["", "This rate would deliver well under the prescribed dose; recheck the conversion of the client's weight to kilograms.", "This rate exceeds the prescribed dose; recheck the concentration of 400 mg in 250 mL.", "This rate would deliver substantially more than 8 mcg/kg/min and risks tachyarrhythmias."]},
    {"id": "n504e3q097", "exam": 4, "topic": "Case: SIRS criteria", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Reviewing P.S.'s assessment, which findings reflect positive systemic inflammatory response syndrome (SIRS) criteria? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["An elevated pulse", "An elevated temperature", "An elevated white blood cell count", "A decreased blood pressure", "An elevated sodium level", "An elevated lactic acid level"], "correct": [0, 1, 2], "rationale": "The SIRS criteria are a temperature above 38 C or below 36 C, a heart rate above 90 beats/min, a respiratory rate above 20 breaths/min or a PaCO2 below 32 mm Hg, and a white count above 12,000 or below 4,000/mm3 or more than 10% bands. P.S. meets three: her temperature is 38.7 C, her pulse is 164 beats/min, and her white count is 18,600/mm3. Her respiratory rate of 18 does not meet the threshold. Her falling blood pressure, high sodium and raised lactate are all clinically important but are not SIRS criteria.", "whyNot": ["", "", "", "Hypotension is a serious finding and a sign of worsening perfusion, but blood pressure is not one of the SIRS criteria.", "Sodium is not a SIRS criterion; it reflects fluid status and is followed for other reasons.", "Lactate supports the picture of tissue hypoperfusion and is central to sepsis care, but it is not itself one of the four SIRS criteria."], "caseId": "ps-uti", "caseTitle": "Case 4 · P.S., urinary tract infection and sepsis", "scenario": "P.S., a 76-year-old woman, was brought to the emergency department after her husband found her sitting on the side of the bed, awake but nonverbal. She has chronic atrial fibrillation, osteoarthritis and hypertension, and takes diltiazem, carvedilol and dabigatran. A STAT CT scan was normal and her stroke score was zero. When she used the bedpan her urine was dark orange, cloudy and strongly odorous, and a specimen was sent for culture. She weighs 198 lb (90 kg). Vital signs on arrival were BP 110/60, P 158, R 20, T 38.2 C (100.8 F); a second set reads BP 98/58, P 164, R 18, T 38.7 C (101.6 F). Labs: sodium 150 mEq/L, potassium 4.8 mEq/L, chloride 100 mEq/L, CO2 25 mEq/L, glucose 104 mg/dL, BUN 10 mg/dL, creatinine 1.2 mg/dL, lactic acid 3.6 mmol/L, WBC 18,600/mm3, hemoglobin 14 g/dL, hematocrit 40.9%, platelets 211,000/mm3."},
    {"id": "n504e3q098", "exam": 4, "topic": "Case: organ dysfunction", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Which finding in P.S.'s presentation most strongly suggests organ dysfunction rather than inflammation alone?", "options": ["The white blood cell count of 18,600/mm3", "Being awake but nonverbal on arrival, with a normal CT scan and a stroke score of zero", "The temperature of 38.7 C", "The dark orange, cloudy, malodorous urine"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "P.S. was found awake but unable to speak, and imaging and the stroke score ruled out a cerebral cause, so the altered mental status points to acute brain dysfunction from her infection. The course identifies sepsis as life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated response to infection, and highlights that in older adults new confusion may be the first sign of sepsis. Her raised lactate of 3.6 mmol/L supports the same conclusion by indicating tissue hypoperfusion.", "whyNot": ["Leukocytosis is a SIRS criterion reflecting inflammation, not evidence that an organ is failing.", "", "Fever is a SIRS criterion and signals the inflammatory response rather than organ dysfunction.", "These characteristics point to the source of infection rather than to dysfunction of an organ system."], "caseId": "ps-uti", "caseTitle": "Case 4 · P.S., urinary tract infection and sepsis", "scenario": "P.S., a 76-year-old woman, was brought to the emergency department after her husband found her sitting on the side of the bed, awake but nonverbal. She has chronic atrial fibrillation, osteoarthritis and hypertension, and takes diltiazem, carvedilol and dabigatran. A STAT CT scan was normal and her stroke score was zero. When she used the bedpan her urine was dark orange, cloudy and strongly odorous, and a specimen was sent for culture. She weighs 198 lb (90 kg). Vital signs on arrival were BP 110/60, P 158, R 20, T 38.2 C (100.8 F); a second set reads BP 98/58, P 164, R 18, T 38.7 C (101.6 F). Labs: sodium 150 mEq/L, potassium 4.8 mEq/L, chloride 100 mEq/L, CO2 25 mEq/L, glucose 104 mg/dL, BUN 10 mg/dL, creatinine 1.2 mg/dL, lactic acid 3.6 mmol/L, WBC 18,600/mm3, hemoglobin 14 g/dL, hematocrit 40.9%, platelets 211,000/mm3."},
    {"id": "n504e3q099", "exam": 4, "topic": "Case: sequencing sepsis orders", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "New orders are written for ceftriaxone 1 g IVPB every 12 hours, blood cultures x 2, lactic acid every 4 hours x 2, a normal saline bolus of 30 mL/kg, and oxygen at 2 L by nasal cannula. The laboratory technician is available now. How should the nurse sequence the ceftriaxone and the blood cultures?", "options": ["Administer the ceftriaxone first, then draw the cultures once the first dose has infused", "Hold the ceftriaxone until the culture and sensitivity results return", "Draw the blood cultures first, then administer the ceftriaxone promptly", "Hold both until P.S.'s temperature comes down with an antipyretic"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Blood cultures are obtained before antimicrobials so the organism can still be identified and the antibiotic narrowed later, and in this scenario the technician is available so no delay is incurred. The course pairs that principle with the firm caution that cultures must not hold up treatment: antimicrobials are ideally given within 1 hour for septic shock or probable sepsis, because delayed administration increases mortality. Collecting promptly and then giving the antibiotic immediately satisfies both requirements.", "whyNot": ["Drawing cultures after the antibiotic has been given reduces the chance of recovering the organism, and the technician is available now.", "Waiting for results delays treatment, and delayed antibiotic administration is linked to increased mortality.", "", "Lowering the fever does nothing to treat the infection and delays both essential interventions."], "caseId": "ps-uti", "caseTitle": "Case 4 · P.S., urinary tract infection and sepsis", "scenario": "P.S., a 76-year-old woman, was brought to the emergency department after her husband found her sitting on the side of the bed, awake but nonverbal. She has chronic atrial fibrillation, osteoarthritis and hypertension, and takes diltiazem, carvedilol and dabigatran. A STAT CT scan was normal and her stroke score was zero. When she used the bedpan her urine was dark orange, cloudy and strongly odorous, and a specimen was sent for culture. She weighs 198 lb (90 kg). Vital signs on arrival were BP 110/60, P 158, R 20, T 38.2 C (100.8 F); a second set reads BP 98/58, P 164, R 18, T 38.7 C (101.6 F). Labs: sodium 150 mEq/L, potassium 4.8 mEq/L, chloride 100 mEq/L, CO2 25 mEq/L, glucose 104 mg/dL, BUN 10 mg/dL, creatinine 1.2 mg/dL, lactic acid 3.6 mmol/L, WBC 18,600/mm3, hemoglobin 14 g/dL, hematocrit 40.9%, platelets 211,000/mm3."},
    {"id": "n504e3q100", "exam": 4, "topic": "Case: fluid bolus calculation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The provider orders a normal saline bolus of 30 mL/kg for P.S. What volume should the nurse plan to infuse?", "options": ["1,350 mL", "1,980 mL", "5,940 mL", "2,700 mL"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "P.S. weighs 198 lb, which is 90 kg, so 30 mL/kg equals 2,700 mL. The course specifies at least 30 mL/kg of crystalloid within 3 hours for hypoperfusion or shock, while emphasizing that therapy is individualized and reassessed often. The purpose of the bolus is to restore effective circulating volume lost to vasodilation and capillary leak, and the nurse monitors MAP, lactate trend, hourly urine output and lung sounds for fluid overload in response.", "whyNot": ["This represents 15 mL/kg, half the ordered dose; recheck the multiplication against her 90-kg weight.", "This figure uses her weight in pounds rather than kilograms at a lower multiplier.", "This result multiplies 30 mL by her weight in pounds instead of kilograms, more than doubling the ordered volume.", ""], "caseId": "ps-uti", "caseTitle": "Case 4 · P.S., urinary tract infection and sepsis", "scenario": "P.S., a 76-year-old woman, was brought to the emergency department after her husband found her sitting on the side of the bed, awake but nonverbal. She has chronic atrial fibrillation, osteoarthritis and hypertension, and takes diltiazem, carvedilol and dabigatran. A STAT CT scan was normal and her stroke score was zero. When she used the bedpan her urine was dark orange, cloudy and strongly odorous, and a specimen was sent for culture. She weighs 198 lb (90 kg). Vital signs on arrival were BP 110/60, P 158, R 20, T 38.2 C (100.8 F); a second set reads BP 98/58, P 164, R 18, T 38.7 C (101.6 F). Labs: sodium 150 mEq/L, potassium 4.8 mEq/L, chloride 100 mEq/L, CO2 25 mEq/L, glucose 104 mg/dL, BUN 10 mg/dL, creatinine 1.2 mg/dL, lactic acid 3.6 mmol/L, WBC 18,600/mm3, hemoglobin 14 g/dL, hematocrit 40.9%, platelets 211,000/mm3."},
    {"id": "n504e3q101", "exam": 4, "topic": "Case: evaluating the lactate trend", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "After fluids and the antibiotic, P.S.'s repeat lactic acid is 3.1 mmol/L, down from 3.6 mmol/L. Her vital signs are now BP 118/72, P 136, R 18, T 37.9 C, and she is awake, alert and asking for dinner. How should the nurse interpret the lactate result?", "options": ["The falling lactate shows improving perfusion and metabolic status, although continued close monitoring is still required", "The value remains abnormal, so the treatment has failed and should be changed", "The normal mental status means the lactate no longer needs to be followed", "The result confirms P.S. no longer meets criteria for sepsis and can be transferred to a general floor"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "A lactate that has fallen from 3.6 to 3.1 mmol/L after fluids and antibiotics shows improving metabolic status, and the course identifies a declining lactate as a good prognostic marker of survival. Her improved mental status and blood pressure support the same conclusion. The material is equally clear that the priority remains close assessment and continuous monitoring for early signs of organ dysfunction, because untreated severe sepsis can still progress to septic shock.", "whyNot": ["", "The course teaches that the trend carries more meaning than a single value, and this trend is moving in the right direction.", "The order specifies repeating the lactate, and the course directs remeasuring an elevated value to follow the trend.", "A single improving value does not reverse the diagnosis; she remains at risk of progressing to septic shock and needs continued close assessment."], "caseId": "ps-uti", "caseTitle": "Case 4 · P.S., urinary tract infection and sepsis", "scenario": "P.S., a 76-year-old woman, was brought to the emergency department after her husband found her sitting on the side of the bed, awake but nonverbal. She has chronic atrial fibrillation, osteoarthritis and hypertension, and takes diltiazem, carvedilol and dabigatran. A STAT CT scan was normal and her stroke score was zero. When she used the bedpan her urine was dark orange, cloudy and strongly odorous, and a specimen was sent for culture. She weighs 198 lb (90 kg). Vital signs on arrival were BP 110/60, P 158, R 20, T 38.2 C (100.8 F); a second set reads BP 98/58, P 164, R 18, T 38.7 C (101.6 F). Labs: sodium 150 mEq/L, potassium 4.8 mEq/L, chloride 100 mEq/L, CO2 25 mEq/L, glucose 104 mg/dL, BUN 10 mg/dL, creatinine 1.2 mg/dL, lactic acid 3.6 mmol/L, WBC 18,600/mm3, hemoglobin 14 g/dL, hematocrit 40.9%, platelets 211,000/mm3."}
  ]
};
